docs.body:
Where the grid's output leaves the page: `downloadBlob` saves an export as a file, `writeClipboard` puts a copy on the clipboard, and `printDocument` prints through a hidden frame. `@gridkitjs/core` builds what each one carries (the file's contents, the copy's text and HTML, the print document); these hand it to the browser.

## Download

`downloadBlob(blob, fileName)` saves `blob` as a file named `fileName` through the browser's own download — a hidden link with a `download` attribute, clicked and removed — so nothing opens or navigates. It is where an export ends: @gridkitjs/core writes the file's contents, the renderer makes a `Blob` of them, and this saves it.

```ts
import { exportFile, resolveExportWriters } from "@gridkitjs/core";
import { downloadBlob } from "@gridkitjs/dom";

const writers = resolveExportWriters(context.options.exportWriters);
const file = await exportFile(context, "csv", {}, writers);
if (file !== null) {
  downloadBlob(
    new Blob([file.content], { type: file.mimeType }),
    file.fileName,
  );
}
```

The link's object URL is revoked a moment after the click, not during it: Safari reads the file from the URL once the click has returned.

## Clipboard

`writeClipboard({ text, html })` puts one copy on the clipboard as both `text/plain` and `text/html`, so a paste into a spreadsheet splits into cells and a paste into a word processor keeps its table. It returns a promise that resolves once the copy is written and rejects when the browser refuses. Call it from a user's gesture — a key press or a menu choice — as browsers only allow the clipboard then.

```ts
import { copyContent } from "@gridkitjs/core";
import { writeClipboard } from "@gridkitjs/dom";

const content = copyContent(context, { kind: "selection" });
if (content !== null) {
  writeClipboard(content).then(
    () => announce(messages.copied(content.count, content.unit)),
    () => announce(messages.copyFailed),
  );
}
```

It tries each way a browser offers, best first:

1. The async Clipboard API with a `ClipboardItem` holding both types.
2. Its `writeText`, plain text alone, where `ClipboardItem` is missing or refused.
3. The legacy `copy` event, for a page outside a secure context where the async API is missing: a one-shot listener sets both types on the event's `clipboardData`, fired by `document.execCommand("copy")` from an off-screen field, and focus goes back where it was.

`isNativeCopy(target)` says whether a copy key pressed at `target` belongs to the browser: pressed in a text field (a text `input`, a `textarea` or editable content), or with text highlighted anywhere on the page. A grid that copies on <Kbd>Ctrl</Kbd>+<Kbd>C</Kbd> checks it first and leaves such a press alone, so the user gets the text they highlighted rather than the cell.

## Print

`printDocument({ title, bodyHtml, styles })` prints a document through a hidden frame: a page of its own holding only `bodyHtml` under `styles`, so what is printed is exactly what was built — every row, not the slice a virtualised grid has drawn — and nothing of the page around it. It returns a promise that resolves once the print dialog has closed and the frame is gone. A frame rather than a new window, which a popup blocker would stop.

```ts
import { buildExportTable, buildPrintDocument } from "@gridkitjs/core";
import { printDocument, printStyles } from "@gridkitjs/dom";

const { title, bodyHtml, page } = buildPrintDocument(
  buildExportTable(context),
  { title: "Orders", timestamp: true },
);
await printDocument({
  title,
  bodyHtml,
  styles: printStyles(page) + ".print-title { color: navy; }",
});
```

The frame is zero-size and `aria-hidden`, appended to `document.body`. Its title and stylesheet are set through the DOM rather than written into its markup, so neither needs escaping. It waits for the document to load and its fonts to be ready, opens the dialog, and is removed on the frame's `afterprint` — or, should a browser never fire it, the next time the user presses a key or the pointer, which a modal print dialog allows only once it has closed. Focus goes back where it was. One print at a time: a call while a print is open resolves at once without printing.

`printStyles(page)` is the stylesheet the frame needs, for a `PrintPage` from `buildPrintDocument`. The frame sees none of the page's CSS, so it carries what makes a table read on paper and nothing of a theme. Pass it as `styles` as it is, add your own rules after it to restyle, or pass a stylesheet of your own in its place.

| Rule                                                                   | What it does                                                                                      |
| ---------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `@page { size: <paperSize> <orientation>; margin: 12mm }`              | The paper and which way it is turned.                                                             |
| `@bottom-right { content: counter(page) " / " counter(pages) }`        | Page numbers, "3 / 12", when `pageNumbers` is on, where the browser draws `@page` margin boxes.   |
| `thead { display: table-header-group }`                                | The header row repeated at the top of every page.                                                 |
| `tfoot { display: table-row-group }`                                   | The grand total once, after the last row, rather than repeated at the foot of every page.         |
| `tr { break-inside: avoid }`                                           | No row split across a page break.                                                                 |
| `table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse }`, 1px `#bbb` borders | The table across the page, with neutral borders.                                                  |
| `.export-group[data-level]`                                            | Group headers shaded and bold, each level stepped in under its parent, as far as six levels deep. |
| `print-color-adjust: exact`                                            | Header and group shading printed as it is, not dropped to save ink.                               |
| `table-layout: fixed`, 8pt, `overflow-wrap: anywhere`                  | For `fitToWidth`: a wide table squeezed onto the page, its columns wrapping their text.           |

## Types

| Type                   | Description                                                                                                                                             |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `ClipboardContent`     | `{ text, html }`: one copy as plain text and as HTML, what `writeClipboard` takes. What `copyContent` from `@gridkitjs/core` returns fits it.           |
| `PrintDocumentOptions` | `{ title, bodyHtml, styles }` for `printDocument`: the document's title as text, the body markup `buildPrintDocument` writes, and the whole stylesheet. |

## See also

- [Core: export](/docs/core/export) for the files, copies and print documents these hand on.
- [Export](/docs/react/export), [clipboard](/docs/react/clipboard) and [printing](/docs/react/printing) for the React grid's use of them.
- [Overview](/docs/dom) for the contract every behaviour follows.

These are the behaviours a renderer attaches to the grid's own table: reading which cell or row a body event addressed (`bodyCellFrom`, `groupRowFrom`, `bodyRowFrom`), the pointer gestures that resize and reorder columns (`startColumnResizeGesture`, `startDragReorder`), the single tab stop the keyboard moves through the grid (`createGridFocus`) and through a long list of values (`attachChecklist`), and the live region that reports what changed (`createLiveAnnouncer`). Each follows [the contract](/docs/dom#the-contract): it attaches to markup the renderer drew and hands back the way to stop it.

## Body event targets

A renderer keeps its rows cheap to re-render by handling the body's clicks, focus and keys with one listener on the `<tbody>` rather than one per cell. These turn such an event's `target` into what it addresses in the grid's markup, `null` when it addresses none:

| Function               | Returns                                                                                                                                       |
| ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `bodyCellFrom(target)` | `{ rowIndex, columnIndex, columnId }` — the data cell (`td[data-gridkit-column]`) it is in. Never a group header's cell, which has no column. |
| `groupRowFrom(target)` | `{ rowIndex, groupId }` — the group-header row (`tr[data-gridkit-group]`) it is in.                                                           |
| `bodyRowFrom(target)`  | `{ rowIndex, columnId }` — any body row it is in, with the column of its cell in that row, or `null` for none — what a row click reports.     |

`rowIndex` is the row's place among every body row, read off its `data-gridkit-row-index`, so it holds under virtualization, where a row's place in the DOM does not.

```ts
import { bodyCellFrom } from "@gridkitjs/dom";

tbody.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
  const cell = bodyCellFrom(event.target);
  if (cell !== null) select(view.pageRows[cell.rowIndex], cell.columnId);
});
```

## Gestures

A gesture starts from a `PointerPress` — the `pointerdown` that began it, native or a framework's wrapper around one — and only from the main button of the primary pointer: a right-click or a second finger starts nothing, and the starter returns `false`. While it runs, the pointer is captured, so the gesture keeps reporting wherever the pointer goes, and <Kbd>Escape</Kbd> cancels it.

### Column resize

`startColumnResizeGesture(handle, press, session, { onMove, onEnd, onCancel })` drags a column's edge. `session` comes from @gridkitjs/core's `beginColumnResize`, which also turns each pointer position into a clamped width. `onMove(width)` reports every move; `onEnd(width)` reports the release, with `null` when the width ended where it started — a click on the handle is not a resize; `onCancel()` reports <Kbd>Escape</Kbd> or a cancelled pointer.

```ts
import { beginColumnResize } from "@gridkitjs/core";
import { startColumnResizeGesture } from "@gridkitjs/dom";

handle.addEventListener("pointerdown", (event) => {
  const session = beginColumnResize(column, width, event.clientX);
  startColumnResizeGesture(handle, event, session, {
    onMove: (width) => controller.resizeColumn(session.columnId, width, "move"),
    onEnd: (width) => {
      if (width !== null)
        controller.resizeColumn(session.columnId, width, "end");
    },
    onCancel: () =>
      controller.cancelColumnResize(
        session.columnId,
        startSizing,
        session.startWidth,
      ),
  });
});
```

`measureColumnContent(table, columnId)` is the width the widest rendered cell of a column needs to show its content in full — what double-clicking a resize handle sizes it to, through `sizeColumnToContent` — or `null` when the column has no cells.

### Drag to reorder

`startDragReorder(id, grabElement, press, { resolveTarget, onState, onDrop })` is the gesture behind every reorder in the grid — a header dragged among the others or into the group-by bar, a group-by chip dragged within it. Nothing is reported until the pointer has travelled 4 pixels, so a press that stays put remains a click; from then on every move asks `resolveTarget(clientX, clientY, id)` where a drop would land and reports `onState({ draggedId, dropTarget, ghostTransform })` — what to draw, with `ghostTransform` a CSS `transform` keeping the ghost under the pointer where it was picked up. Release over a target calls `onDrop(target, id)`; every ending reports `onState(NO_DRAG)`.

```ts
import {
  NO_DRAG,
  resolveColumnDropTarget,
  startDragReorder,
} from "@gridkitjs/dom";

header.addEventListener("pointerdown", (event) => {
  startDragReorder(columnId, header, event, {
    resolveTarget: (x, y, id) =>
      resolveColumnDropTarget(x, y, id, {
        order,
        groupBy,
        groupByDraggableIds,
      }),
    onState: (state) => draw(state),
    onDrop: (target, id) =>
      target.kind === "group-by"
        ? controller.moveGroupBy(id, target.beforeColumnId)
        : controller.moveColumn(id, target.beforeId),
  });
});
```

The grid's own targets are hit-tested against its markup:

| Function                                           | Returns                                                                                                                                                                                            |
| -------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `resolveColumnDropTarget(x, y, movedId, context)`  | `{ kind: "column-order", beforeId }` over another `th[data-gridkit-column]`, by which half the pointer is in; else a group-by target for a column that may be grouped and is not yet; else `null`. |
| `resolveGroupByDropTarget(x, y, movedId, groupBy)` | `{ kind: "group-by", beforeColumnId }` over `[data-gridkit-group-by-bar]`, before or after the `[data-gridkit-group-chip]` under the pointer, or appended; `null` elsewhere.                       |

Either returns `null` for a drop that would change nothing, so a drop indicator only ever promises a move that happens.

## Focus movement

A grid is one tab stop: <Kbd>Tab</Kbd> reaches it and passes it by, and the arrow keys, <Kbd>Home</Kbd>, <Kbd>End</Kbd>, <Kbd>PageUp</Kbd> and <Kbd>PageDown</Kbd> move within it. `createGridFocus({ rowCount, columnCount, isSkippableRow, virtualRange })` keeps that tab stop — starting on the header's first cell — and moves the browser's focus with it.

| Member                                 | Description                                                                                                                                   |
| -------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `getFocus()`                           | The focused cell, `{ rowIndex, columnIndex }` with `HEADER_ROW` (`-1`) for the header, clamped to the counts. The same object until it moves. |
| `subscribe(listener)`                  | Calls `listener` when the focused cell moves.                                                                                                 |
| `setOptions(options)`                  | The current counts, skippable rows and virtualized window, given on every render. Calls no listener.                                          |
| `attach({ getHeaderRow, getBodyRow })` | Connects it to the rendered rows; returns the function that disconnects them.                                                                 |
| `focusCell(rowIndex, columnIndex)`     | Moves the tab stop. Moving to the focused cell does nothing.                                                                                  |
| `handleKey(key, modifiers, pageSize)`  | Moves the tab stop as a navigation key does, and returns `true` — prevent the key's default then — or returns `false` for any other key.      |
| `focusDrawnCell(drawn)`                | Called after drawing `drawn`, a value `getFocus()` returned: moves the browser's focus to that cell if a move to it is pending.               |

```ts
import { HEADER_ROW } from "@gridkitjs/core";
import { createGridFocus, findBodyRow, measurePageSize } from "@gridkitjs/dom";

const focus = createGridFocus({ rowCount, columnCount });
focus.attach({
  getHeaderRow: () => headerTable.tHead?.rows[0] ?? null,
  getBodyRow: (rowIndex) => findBodyRow(bodyTable, rowIndex),
});
focus.subscribe(() => draw()); // draw() renders tabindex 0 on focus.getFocus(), then calls focus.focusDrawnCell(focus.getFocus())

grid.addEventListener("keydown", (event) => {
  if (focus.handleKey(event.key, event, measurePageSize(bodyTable))) {
    event.preventDefault();
  }
});
```

The browser's focus follows only once the move is drawn, so it lands on the cell in the new markup — after a column moves, the header now at that index. A virtualized row outside the drawn window is scrolled into it with `virtualRange.scrollToIndex` and focused when a later drawing mounts it. `findBodyRow(bodyTable, rowIndex)` finds a body row by its `data-gridkit-row-index`, not its position, which under virtualization is off by the rows not mounted; `measurePageSize(bodyTable)` is how many rows a page key moves — as many as fit the body's scrolling area.

## Checklist

`attachChecklist(list, options)` makes a long multi-select list the renderer has drawn — a filter's distinct values, thousands of them, usually virtualized so only a window is mounted — behave as one with a single tab stop, after the [WAI-ARIA listbox pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/listbox/). The keys move focus among its items, and <Kbd>Space</Kbd> or a click asks for an item to be toggled. It decides what each key does; the renderer flips the item in its own state and draws the result. It returns `setCount(count)`, `focusItem(index)` and `detach()`.

The behaviour sets nothing on the markup but `tabindex`. The roles, the label and the checked state are the renderer's to draw:

| Markup                                                        | On        | Meaning                                                                                                                     |
| ------------------------------------------------------------- | --------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `role="listbox"`, `aria-multiselectable="true"`               | the list  | A list of values any number of which can be checked. Give it an `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby` too.                      |
| `role="option"`, `aria-checked`                               | each item | A value, and whether it is checked — `"true"` or `"false"`, redrawn from the caller's state after each `onToggle`.          |
| `aria-setsize`, `aria-posinset`                               | each item | How many items the whole list holds and this one's place in it, from 1, since only a window of them is in the page.         |
| `data-gridkit-checklist-item`, `data-gridkit-checklist-index` | each item | What makes it an item, and its place in the whole list from 0 — the index every callback reports. Read afresh on every key. |

The list is the element that scrolls — the virtualizer's scroll element — so put the spacers that stand in for unmounted items inside it with `role="presentation"`. One item at a time has `tabindex="0"` — the focused one, or the first mounted while the focused one is scrolled away — and the rest `-1`; an observer of the list keeps that so as items mount and unmount, so do not render a `tabindex` of your own.

| Option          | Called                                                                                                                                        |
| --------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `onToggle`      | `(index)` — <Kbd>Space</Kbd> on an item, or a click on it. Flip it and draw its new `aria-checked`.                                           |
| `onFocusChange` | `(index)` — focus moved to another item, by a key, a click, <Kbd>Tab</Kbd> or `focusItem`. Optional.                                          |
| `scrollToIndex` | `(index)` — bring the item into view, mounting it if the window does not hold it. Forward it to the virtualizer, with `{ block: "nearest" }`. |

| Key                                       | Does                                                                                           |
| ----------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| <Kbd>ArrowDown</Kbd> / <Kbd>ArrowUp</Kbd> | Focuses the next or previous item, stopping at the ends.                                       |
| <Kbd>Home</Kbd> / <Kbd>End</Kbd>          | Focuses the first or last item of the whole list.                                              |
| <Kbd>PageDown</Kbd> / <Kbd>PageUp</Kbd>   | Moves focus by as many items as the list shows whole.                                          |
| <Kbd>Space</Kbd>                          | `onToggle(index)` for the focused item, once however long it is held.                          |
| <Kbd>Enter</Kbd>                          | Not handled, so a form or panel around the list hears it — a filter popup applies.             |
| <Kbd>Escape</Kbd>                         | Not handled here: the [overlay stack](/docs/dom/overlays#stacking) closes the topmost overlay. |

A key pressed with <Kbd>Ctrl</Kbd>, <Kbd>Alt</Kbd>, <Kbd>Shift</Kbd> or <Kbd>Meta</Kbd> is left alone. A move to an item outside the window asks `scrollToIndex` for it and focuses it once it mounts, unless focus has gone elsewhere in the meantime. There is no typeahead: a checklist of values comes with a search box.

`setCount(count)` says how many items the whole list holds. It starts at 0, so call it straight after attaching, and again whenever the list changes — a search narrowing it, say; a focused place past the new end moves back to the last item. `focusItem(index)` focuses an item from code, scrolling to it first.

```ts
import { attachChecklist, createRowVirtualizer } from "@gridkitjs/dom";

// <div role="listbox" aria-multiselectable="true" aria-label="Values"
//      style="height: 240px; overflow: auto">
//   <div role="presentation" style="height: {offsetBefore}px"></div>
//   <div role="option" aria-checked="false" aria-setsize="5000"
//        aria-posinset="1" data-gridkit-checklist-item
//        data-gridkit-checklist-index="0">(Blanks)</div>
//   …the rest of the window…
//   <div role="presentation" style="height: {offsetAfter}px"></div>
// </div>
const virtualizer = createRowVirtualizer(listElement, {
  estimatedRowHeight: 28,
  overscan: 4,
  onRange: (range) => drawItems(range),
});
const checklist = attachChecklist(listElement, {
  onToggle: (index) => toggleValue(index), // then redraw its aria-checked
  scrollToIndex: (index) => {
    virtualizer.scrollToIndex(index, { block: "nearest" });
  },
});
virtualizer.setRowCount(items.length);
checklist.setCount(items.length);
```

A "Select all" box belongs above the list as a native checkbox of its own, with its own tab stop and `indeterminate` while some items are checked, rather than as an option inside it.

## Live announcer

`createLiveAnnouncer(container?)` adds one visually hidden live region (`role="status"`, `aria-live="polite"`) as the last child of `container` — `document.body` when omitted — and returns `announce(text)` and `destroy()`. The region carries the `gridkit-sr-only` class, which @gridkitjs/theme-tailwind clips to nothing while keeping it in the accessibility tree.

```ts
import { createLiveAnnouncer } from "@gridkitjs/dom";

const announcer = createLiveAnnouncer(gridElement);
announcer.announce("Amount, column 2 of 5");
announcer.announce("Amount, column 2 of 5"); // read out again
announcer.destroy();
```

A live region speaks only when its text changes, so `announce` makes a repeat of the last message a change of its own: the same text twice in a row is read twice.

## Types

| Type                          | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                        |
| ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `BodyCellTarget`              | `{ rowIndex, columnIndex, columnId }`, what `bodyCellFrom` returns. `rowIndex` is read off `data-gridkit-row-index`; `columnIndex` is the cell's place in its row.                                                 |
| `GroupRowTarget`              | `{ rowIndex, groupId }`, what `groupRowFrom` returns.                                                                                                                                                              |
| `BodyRowTarget`               | `{ rowIndex, columnId }`, what `bodyRowFrom` returns. `columnId` is `null` when the event was in no cell.                                                                                                          |
| `PointerPress`                | The part of a `pointerdown` a gesture reads: `pointerId`, `clientX`, `clientY`, `button`, `isPrimary` and `preventDefault()`. A native `PointerEvent` fits, and so does React's synthetic one.                     |
| `ColumnResizeGestureHandlers` | `{ onMove, onEnd, onCancel }`, the last argument of `startColumnResizeGesture`, described under [column resize](#column-resize).                                                                                   |
| `DragReorderState<Target>`    | `{ draggedId, dropTarget, ghostTransform }`, what `onState` reports while a reorder drag is open. `NO_DRAG` is the state with all three `null`.                                                                    |
| `DragReorderHandlers<Target>` | `{ resolveTarget, onState, onDrop }`, the last argument of `startDragReorder`.                                                                                                                                     |
| `ColumnDropTarget`            | Where a dragged header would land: `{ kind: "column-order", beforeId }` among the headers (`beforeId` is `null` for the end), or a `GroupByDropTarget`.                                                            |
| `GroupByDropTarget`           | `{ kind: "group-by", beforeColumnId }`: a drop into the group-by bar, in front of `beforeColumnId`, or appended when it is `null`.                                                                                 |
| `ColumnDropContext`           | `{ order, groupBy, groupByDraggableIds }`, the last argument of `resolveColumnDropTarget`: the column order on screen, the current group-by state, and the ids whose header may join the group-by bar.             |
| `GridFocusOptions`            | `{ rowCount, columnCount, isSkippableRow?, virtualRange? }` for `createGridFocus` and its `setOptions`. `rowCount` counts group headers and summary rows; `isSkippableRow` marks rows the vertical keys step over. |
| `GridFocusController`         | What `createGridFocus` returns. Its members are tabled under [focus movement](#focus-movement).                                                                                                                    |
| `GridFocusRows`               | `{ getHeaderRow, getBodyRow }`, passed to `attach`: how to find the rendered header row, and body row `rowIndex`, each `null` while it is not rendered.                                                            |
| `FocusVirtualRange`           | `{ startIndex, endIndex, scrollToIndex }`: the window of rows a virtualizer drew, and how to bring a row into it.                                                                                                  |
| `ChecklistOptions`            | `{ onToggle, onFocusChange?, scrollToIndex }` for `attachChecklist`, tabled under [checklist](#checklist).                                                                                                         |
| `AttachedChecklist`           | `{ setCount, focusItem, detach }`, what `attachChecklist` returns.                                                                                                                                                 |
| `LiveAnnouncer`               | `{ announce, destroy }`, what `createLiveAnnouncer` returns.                                                                                                                                                       |

## See also

- [Overview](/docs/dom) for the contract every behaviour follows.
- [Overlays](/docs/dom/overlays) for the panel a checklist is shown in, whose stack handles <Kbd>Escape</Kbd>.
- [Observers and virtualizer](/docs/dom/observers-and-virtualizer) for the row virtualizer `virtualRange` comes from.
- [Core: focus and virtualization](/docs/core/navigation) for the key handling `handleKey` applies.
- [Core: resizing](/docs/core/resizing) and [core: ordering](/docs/core/ordering) for the state these gestures change.
- [Accessibility](/docs/react/accessibility) for the keyboard model the React grid builds on them.

Two keyboard patterns a grid's menus and toolbar follow, each attached to markup the renderer drew: `attachMenu` for a menu and its submenus, and `attachToolbar` for a toolbar, with `observeToolbarOverflow` working out which entries fit. Each decides what a key or a click does and reports it; the renderer changes its own state and draws the result. A menu is shown in an [overlay](/docs/dom/overlays), whose stack closes the topmost one on <Kbd>Escape</Kbd>. The checklist a filter popup shows is on [gestures and focus](/docs/dom/gestures-and-focus#checklist).

## Menu

`attachMenu(panel, options)` makes a menu the renderer has drawn behave as one, following the [WAI-ARIA menu pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/menubar/): keys, typeahead and the pointer move focus among its items, and choosing one is reported through callbacks. It decides what each key and hover does; the renderer runs the item, opens or closes the submenu, and draws the result. It returns `focusItem(idOrIndex)` and `detach()`.

What to show is @gridkitjs/core's: [`resolveMenu`](/docs/core/commands#menus) resolves a grid's menu entries to the items to draw, and the keys below are worked out with its [pure helpers](/docs/core/commands#keyboard). `attachMenu` reads only the markup:

| Markup                                                       | Read as                                                                                                                                             |
| ------------------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `role="menuitem"`, `"menuitemcheckbox"` or `"menuitemradio"` | An item, when it also carries `data-gridkit-menu-item`. Its value is the id every callback reports. Read afresh on every key, so later items count. |
| `aria-haspopup="menu"`                                       | An item that opens a submenu. Give it `aria-expanded` too, `"true"` while the submenu is open.                                                      |
| `aria-disabled="true"`                                       | A disabled item: it takes focus, so a screen reader finds it, but is never chosen.                                                                  |
| `role="separator"`, or anything else                         | Not an item, so the keys pass over it.                                                                                                              |
| `aria-label`, else the text                                  | The label typeahead matches.                                                                                                                        |

It keeps one item the tab stop — `tabindex="0"` on the focused item, or the first, `-1` on the rest — so attach the menu before its overlay, and the overlay's initial focus lands on the first item.

| Option              | Called                                                                                                                                                                                                       |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `onActivate`        | `(id)` — an enabled item that opens no submenu was chosen. Run it and close every menu.                                                                                                                      |
| `onOpenSubmenu`     | `(id, { focus })` — an enabled submenu item asks for its submenu. `focus` is `"first"` from the keyboard, so focus its first item; `"none"` from a click or the pointer resting on it, so leave focus there. |
| `onCollapseSubmenu` | `()` — the pointer came to rest on another item. Close any submenu open from this menu.                                                                                                                      |
| `onCloseSubmenu`    | `()` — <Kbd>ArrowLeft</Kbd> in this menu, which is a submenu: close it. Pass it only to a submenu's menu.                                                                                                    |
| `onClose`           | `("tab")` — <Kbd>Tab</Kbd> or <Kbd>Shift</Kbd>+<Kbd>Tab</Kbd>: close every menu, and focus returns to where the first was opened from.                                                                       |

| Key                                              | Does                                                                                                                                                        |
| ------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| <Kbd>ArrowDown</Kbd> / <Kbd>ArrowUp</Kbd>        | Focuses the next or previous item, wrapping round the ends.                                                                                                 |
| <Kbd>Home</Kbd> / <Kbd>End</Kbd>                 | Focuses the first or last item.                                                                                                                             |
| <Kbd>Enter</Kbd> / <Kbd>Space</Kbd>              | `onActivate(id)`; on a submenu item, `onOpenSubmenu(id, { focus: "first" })`. Nothing on a disabled item.                                                   |
| <Kbd>ArrowRight</Kbd>                            | On a submenu item, `onOpenSubmenu(id, { focus: "first" })` — also for one already open, when focus should move into it.                                     |
| <Kbd>ArrowLeft</Kbd>                             | `onCloseSubmenu()`, when given.                                                                                                                             |
| <Kbd>Tab</Kbd> / <Kbd>Shift</Kbd>+<Kbd>Tab</Kbd> | `onClose("tab")`, in place of the key's own move.                                                                                                           |
| A printable character                            | Typeahead: focuses the next item whose label starts with what was typed in the last half second. One letter typed again steps through every item it starts. |
| <Kbd>Escape</Kbd>                                | Not handled here: the [overlay stack](/docs/dom/overlays#stacking) closes the topmost overlay.                                                              |

Moving the pointer over an item focuses it at once. What the item opens or closes waits until the pointer has rested on it for 150 ms: a submenu item reports `onOpenSubmenu(id, { focus: "none" })`, any other item `onCollapseSubmenu()`. Leaving the panel cancels the wait, so a pointer crossing another item on its way into an open submenu leaves it open. A click chooses an item as <Kbd>Enter</Kbd> does, except that a submenu opens with `focus: "none"`. Touch has no hover: only its taps count, as clicks.

### Submenus

A submenu is a menu of its own: a panel with its own `attachMenu`, shown in an overlay of its own anchored to its item. Attach it when it opens, after the menu below it, so it sits above that menu in the [stack](/docs/dom/overlays#stacking): <Kbd>Escape</Kbd> then closes the submenu alone, focus goes back to its item, and closing the menu closes the submenu with it.

```ts
import {
  attachMenu,
  attachOverlay,
  type AttachedMenu,
  type MenuSubmenuFocus,
} from "@gridkitjs/dom";

// <div role="menu" aria-label="Column"> holding, among its items,
// <div role="menuitem" data-gridkit-menu-item="grouping"
//      aria-haspopup="menu" aria-expanded="false">Grouping</div>
function showMenu(
  anchor: HTMLElement,
  menu: HTMLElement,
  submenu: HTMLElement,
) {
  const groupingItem = menu.querySelector<HTMLElement>(
    '[data-gridkit-menu-item="grouping"]',
  )!;
  let openSubmenu: { behaviour: AttachedMenu; close: () => void } | null = null;

  function closeSubmenu() {
    openSubmenu?.close();
  }

  function showSubmenu(focus: MenuSubmenuFocus) {
    if (openSubmenu !== null) {
      if (focus === "first") openSubmenu.behaviour.focusItem(0);
      return;
    }
    document.body.append(submenu);
    groupingItem.setAttribute("aria-expanded", "true");
    const behaviour = attachMenu(submenu, {
      onActivate: (id) => {
        run(id);
        close();
      },
      onCloseSubmenu: closeSubmenu,
      onClose: close,
    });
    const detach = attachOverlay(groupingItem, submenu, {
      placement: "right-start",
      offset: -4, // the submenu's top padding, so its first item lines up
      initialFocus: focus,
      onDismiss: closeSubmenu,
    });
    openSubmenu = {
      behaviour,
      close: () => {
        openSubmenu = null;
        detach();
        behaviour.detach();
        submenu.remove();
        groupingItem.setAttribute("aria-expanded", "false");
      },
    };
  }

  document.body.append(menu);
  const behaviour = attachMenu(menu, {
    onActivate: (id) => {
      run(id);
      close();
    },
    onOpenSubmenu: (_id, { focus }) => showSubmenu(focus),
    onCollapseSubmenu: closeSubmenu,
    onClose: close,
  });
  const detach = attachOverlay(anchor, menu, { onDismiss: close });

  function close() {
    detach(); // dismisses the submenu above it with "parent" first
    behaviour.detach();
    menu.remove();
  }
}
```

## Toolbar

`attachToolbar(toolbar)` makes a `role="toolbar"` element the renderer has drawn one tab stop that the arrow keys move within, following the [WAI-ARIA toolbar pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/toolbar/), and returns the function that stops it. What the toolbar lists is @gridkitjs/core's: [`resolveToolbar`](/docs/core/toolbar#resolving) resolves a grid's toolbar entries to the items to draw.

| Markup                      | Read as                                                                                                                                                                                                      |
| --------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `data-gridkit-toolbar-item` | An entry, holding the item's `key`. Its control is the entry itself when it can take focus, else the first element in it that can — a search box's input, a custom entry's button. Read afresh on every key. |
| An entry with no control    | A separator or a spacer: the keys pass over it.                                                                                                                                                              |
| `aria-disabled="true"`      | A disabled control: it still takes focus, so a screen reader finds it. Running it is the renderer's to refuse.                                                                                               |

One control at a time has `tabindex="0"` — the focused one, or the first — and the rest `-1`, so <Kbd>Tab</Kbd> reaches the toolbar once and returns to the control last used. A control drawn later, such as a button whose command became visible, joins with `-1`, leaving the tab stop where it was. Leave `tabindex` to it.

| Key                                          | Does                                                           |
| -------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |
| <Kbd>ArrowRight</Kbd> / <Kbd>ArrowLeft</Kbd> | Focuses the next or previous control, wrapping round the ends. |
| <Kbd>Home</Kbd> / <Kbd>End</Kbd>             | Focuses the first or last control.                             |

A text field keeps the keys it needs: <Kbd>Home</Kbd> and <Kbd>End</Kbd> move its caret, and so do the arrows while there is text to move through. With the caret at the start, <Kbd>ArrowLeft</Kbd> moves on to the previous control; at the end, <Kbd>ArrowRight</Kbd> to the next — otherwise a search box holding the tab stop would leave no way to the controls after it. With text selected, or in a field that keeps its caret to itself, such as `type="email"`, the arrows stay with the field.

```ts
import { attachToolbar } from "@gridkitjs/dom";

// <div role="toolbar" aria-label="Grid toolbar">
//   <div data-gridkit-toolbar-item="search"><input type="search" … /></div>
//   <div data-gridkit-toolbar-item="spacer-1"></div>
//   <button data-gridkit-toolbar-item="filter.clearAll" aria-disabled="true">Clear filters</button>
// </div>
const detach = attachToolbar(toolbarElement);
```

### Overflow

`observeToolbarOverflow(toolbar, { items, onChange })` measures how many of a toolbar's entries fit in it, and reports the count — what @gridkitjs/core's [`fitToolbarItems`](/docs/core/toolbar#overflow) works out from the widths — through `onChange`: once straight away, so a renderer calling it before paint draws the right entries from the first frame, and again whenever it changes. Draw the first `count` entries and every pinned one — or let [`splitToolbarItems`](/docs/core/toolbar#overflow) split them — and put the rest in a "More" menu button at the end. It returns `update(options)` and `detach()`.

| Option     | Type                             | Description                                                                                                |
| ---------- | -------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `items`    | `readonly ToolbarOverflowItem[]` | Every entry, drawn or in "More", in order: `{ type, key, label? }`, as `resolveToolbar`'s items have them. |
| `onChange` | `(count) => void`                | How many entries fit, from the start. Called straight away, then only when it changes.                     |

| Markup                      | Read as                                                                        |
| --------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `data-gridkit-toolbar-item` | An entry, whose value is its item's `key`.                                     |
| `data-gridkit-toolbar-more` | The "More" button — an entry too, for `attachToolbar`, but not one of `items`. |

An entry moved into "More" is no longer drawn, so its width is remembered by key from the last time it was. One never drawn yet counts as nothing: it is drawn, measured, and moved back out if it does not fit. So does one whose `label` changed, as `update` finds it, while it was in "More": its remembered width is for the old label. The "More" button likewise counts as nothing until it is first drawn. A spacer counts as nothing too, since its width is only the room left over. The toolbar's padding and the `column-gap` between entries are allowed for, so a theme can space them however it likes.

It measures again whenever the toolbar or an entry resizes, and whenever entries are drawn or taken away — those the count it reported lets in, or the "More" button — so the renderer only draws what it is told. When the items change, pass them to `update`.

```ts
import { resolveToolbar, splitToolbarItems } from "@gridkitjs/core";
import { observeToolbarOverflow } from "@gridkitjs/dom";

const overflow = observeToolbarOverflow(toolbarElement, {
  items,
  onChange: (count) => draw(splitToolbarItems(items, count)), // { shown, overflow }
});

// when the grid changes:
items = resolveToolbar(entries, { commands, contextFor });
overflow.update({
  items,
  onChange: (count) => draw(splitToolbarItems(items, count)),
});
```

## Types

| Type                     | Description                                                                                                    |
| ------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `MenuOptions`            | `{ onActivate, onOpenSubmenu?, onCollapseSubmenu?, onCloseSubmenu?, onClose }` for `attachMenu`, tabled above. |
| `AttachedMenu`           | `{ focusItem, detach }`, what `attachMenu` returns. `focusItem` takes an item's id or its index.               |
| `MenuSubmenuFocus`       | `"first"` or `"none"`: whether a submenu opens with focus on its first item, or leaves focus where it is.      |
| `MenuCloseReason`        | `"tab"`, the one reason `onClose` reports.                                                                     |
| `ToolbarOverflowOptions` | `{ items, onChange }` for `observeToolbarOverflow` and its `update`.                                           |
| `ToolbarOverflowItem`    | `{ type, key, label? }`: one toolbar entry as the overflow reads it.                                           |
| `ToolbarOverflow`        | `{ update, detach }`, what `observeToolbarOverflow` returns.                                                   |

