## Props

| Prop                                 | Type                                 | Default | Description                                                                                                                                                                  |
| ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------ | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `resizableColumns`                   | `boolean`                            | `false` | Whether columns can be dragged wider, unless a column says otherwise.                                                                                                        |
| `resizeMode`                         | `"fit" \| "fixed"`                   | `"fit"` | Whether columns fill the grid's width or sit at their own.                                                                                                                   |
| `columnSizeDefaults`                 | `Partial<ColumnSizeDefaults>`        |         | Width, minWidth, maxWidth applied to a column that sets none of its own.                                                                                                     |
| `defaultColumnSizing`                | `ColumnSizingState`                  |         | Column widths to start from, keyed by column id. Uncontrolled.                                                                                                               |
| `columnSizing`                       | `ColumnSizingState`                  |         | Column widths, controlled — a drag or nudge asks through `onColumnResize` (every `"move"` too), whose `sizing` is the next state. See [controlled state](/docs/react/state). |
| `onColumnResize`                     | `(event: ColumnResizeEvent) => void` |         | Called as the user resizes a column: continuously with phase "move", once with "end".                                                                                        |
| `column.resizable`                   | `boolean`                            |         | Per-column override of resizableColumns — a column can opt out of an otherwise resizable grid, or in on one that is not.                                                     |
| `column.width / minWidth / maxWidth` | `number`                             |         | This column's own starting width and resize bounds.                                                                                                                          |

## Keyboard & pointer interactions

- Drag a header's trailing edge to resize; double-click it to size the column to its content.
- With a resizable header focused, <Kbd>Alt</Kbd>+<Kbd>ArrowLeft</Kbd> / <Kbd>Alt</Kbd>+<Kbd>ArrowRight</Kbd> nudge the width and <Kbd>Alt</Kbd>+<Kbd>Enter</Kbd> sizes the column to its content, as double-clicking the edge does; <Kbd>Escape</Kbd> cancels an in-progress resize.

## Autofit

Sizing a column to its content — a double-click on its edge, <Kbd>Alt</Kbd>+<Kbd>Enter</Kbd> on its header — measures the column's content in the rows on screen and fits the column to it, within its `minWidth`/`maxWidth`, firing `onColumnResize` once with `"end"`. On a paginated or virtualized grid, rows not drawn are not measured. The same fit is available from code: the ref's [`autoFitColumn(columnId)`](/docs/react/imperative-handle#actions) and `autoFitColumns(columnIds?)`, which fits every column shown when given none, and the `column.autoFit` and `column.autoFitAll` commands, which a [context menu](/docs/react/context-menu#default-items) or [toolbar](/docs/react/toolbar) can carry or `runCommand` can run.

## Reading column widths outside the grid's own tree

`useColumnSizingState` subscribes to the grid's column widths through its `ref`, for a component living elsewhere on the page:

```tsx title="App.tsx"
import { useRef, type RefObject } from "react";
import {
  DataGrid,
  useColumnSizingState,
  type ColumnDefinition,
  type DataGridApi,
} from "@gridkitjs/react";

interface Book {
  id: number;
  title: string;
  author: string;
}

const books: Book[] = [
  { id: 1, title: "Kindred", author: "Butler" },
  { id: 2, title: "The Dispossessed", author: "Le Guin" },
];

const columns: readonly ColumnDefinition<Book>[] = [
  { field: "title", header: "Title" },
  { field: "author", header: "Author" },
];

function WidthReadout({
  gridRef,
}: {
  gridRef: RefObject<DataGridApi<Book> | null>;
}) {
  const sizing = useColumnSizingState(gridRef);
  return <p>{Object.keys(sizing).length} columns resized</p>;
}

export default function App() {
  const gridRef = useRef<DataGridApi<Book>>(null);
  return (
    <>
      <WidthReadout gridRef={gridRef} />
      <DataGrid
        ref={gridRef}
        columns={columns}
        dataSource={books}
        getRowId={(row) => String(row.id)}
        label="Books"
        resizableColumns
      />
    </>
  );
}
```

`useColumnSizingState` only reads. To change column widths from code, call the ref's `setColumnSizing(sizing)`, which fires `onColumnResize`, or hold it yourself with the controlled `columnSizing` prop. Before the grid mounts, the hook reads as an empty object. See [imperative handle](/docs/react/imperative-handle) for `getColumnSizing` and `subscribe`.

## See also

- [Column reordering](/docs/react/column-reordering) for moving columns.
- [Column definitions](/docs/react/column-definition) for `width`, `minWidth` and `maxWidth`.
- [core: Resizing](/docs/core/resizing) for the framework-free logic.

`sortableColumns` turns on a clickable sort toggle in every header, unless a column sets its own `sortable` to override the grid. A plain click sorts by that column alone, cycling ascending, descending, and off; <Kbd>Shift</Kbd>+click adds or updates the column in a priority-ordered stack instead of replacing it — sort by `Id` ascending, then by `Application.Name` descending as a tiebreaker.

## Click to sort, Shift-click to stack

<ColumnSortingExample />

## Props

| Prop                 | Type                                 | Default | Description                                                                                                                                                                                       |
| -------------------- | ------------------------------------ | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `sortableColumns`    | `boolean`                            | `false` | Whether columns can be sorted by clicking their header toggle.                                                                                                                                    |
| `defaultColumnSort`  | `readonly { columnId, direction }[]` |         | The sort to start with, in priority order. Uncontrolled.                                                                                                                                          |
| `columnSort`         | `ColumnSortState`                    |         | The sort, controlled: a header click, the ref's `setColumnSort` or a sort command asks for a change through `onColumnSortChange` instead of making it. See [controlled state](/docs/react/state). |
| `onColumnSortChange` | `(event: ColumnSortEvent) => void`   |         | Called once when the user changes the sort — a toggle, a stack, or a clear back to "none".                                                                                                        |
| `column.sortable`    | `boolean`                            |         | Per-column override of sortableColumns.                                                                                                                                                           |

## Keyboard & pointer interactions

- Click a header's sort toggle to cycle it through ascending, descending, and off.
- <Kbd>Shift</Kbd>+click a toggle to add or update that column in the sort's
  stack instead of replacing it.
- With a header focused, <Kbd>Alt</Kbd>+<Kbd>ArrowUp</Kbd> cycles its sort the same way a click does; <Kbd>Alt</Kbd>+<Kbd>Shift</Kbd>+<Kbd>ArrowUp</Kbd> stacks it the same way a <Kbd>Shift</Kbd>+click does.

## Reading sort state outside the grid's own tree

`useColumnSortState` subscribes to the grid's active sort through its `ref`, for a sort indicator living elsewhere on the page:

```tsx title="App.tsx"
import { useRef, type RefObject } from "react";
import {
  DataGrid,
  useColumnSortState,
  type ColumnDefinition,
  type DataGridApi,
} from "@gridkitjs/react";

interface Book {
  id: number;
  title: string;
  author: string;
  year: number;
}

const books: Book[] = [
  { id: 1, title: "The Left Hand of Darkness", author: "Le Guin", year: 1969 },
  { id: 2, title: "Kindred", author: "Butler", year: 1979 },
  { id: 3, title: "The Dispossessed", author: "Le Guin", year: 1974 },
];

const columns: readonly ColumnDefinition<Book>[] = [
  { field: "title", header: "Title" },
  { field: "author", header: "Author" },
  { field: "year", header: "Year", type: "number" },
];

function SortIndicator({
  gridRef,
}: {
  gridRef: RefObject<DataGridApi<Book> | null>;
}) {
  const sort = useColumnSortState(gridRef);
  if (sort.length === 0) {
    return <p>Not sorted</p>;
  }
  return (
    <p>
      Sorted by{" "}
      {sort.map((entry) => `${entry.columnId} ${entry.direction}`).join(", ")}
    </p>
  );
}

export default function App() {
  const gridRef = useRef<DataGridApi<Book>>(null);
  return (
    <>
      <SortIndicator gridRef={gridRef} />
      <DataGrid
        ref={gridRef}
        columns={columns}
        dataSource={books}
        getRowId={(row) => String(row.id)}
        label="Books"
        sortableColumns
      />
    </>
  );
}
```

`useColumnSortState` only reads. To change the sort from code, call the ref's `setColumnSort(sort)`, which replaces it and fires `onColumnSortChange`, or run a sort command — `runCommand("sort.ascending", { area: "header", columnId })`, likewise `"sort.descending"` and `"sort.clear"`, or `"sort.clearAll"` with no target — as the [context menu](/docs/react/context-menu#default-items) does; to hold the sort yourself, pass the controlled `columnSort` prop, which is also how a [server sorts the rows](/docs/react/server-side-data). Before the grid mounts, the hook reads as an empty array. See [imperative handle](/docs/react/imperative-handle) for `getColumnSort`/`subscribe`, and [row grouping](/docs/react/row-grouping) for `useGroupByState`'s equivalent.

