- [core: Sorting](/docs/core/sorting) for the framework-free logic and the comparators.
- [Column filter menu](/docs/react/column-filter-menu) for the sort items in the header menu.
- [Server-side data](/docs/react/server-side-data) for sorting on a server.
- [Controlled and uncontrolled state](/docs/react/state) and [imperative handle](/docs/react/imperative-handle) for `columnSort`, `getColumnSort` and `subscribe`.

`headerTemplate` and `cellTemplate` draw a header or a cell with any React node, so markup and conditional styling are plain component code. Start from `defineColumnsFromRows` for the columns you don't need to customize, then declare the ones you do.

## When you need a template

A column without a template already shows its value as text — formatted through `Intl` (dates, numbers, currencies, percentages) once it has a `format` or the grid a `locale` — and a header without one shows the column's `header` text. So reach for `format` or `valueFormatter` when only the text should change, and `header` when the header is just a name; see [formatting values](/docs/react/column-definition#formatting-values).

A template is for markup: a link or a button in a cell, a badge, a style that depends on the row, an icon in a header. A `cellTemplate` receives the raw `value`, not the formatted text. A `headerTemplate` can sit beside a `header`: the template is what the header shows, and `header` is what screen-reader announcements call the column, since markup need not be text.

## Formatted currency column

<ColumnTemplatesExample />

## The `cellTemplate` context

`cellTemplate` receives a single `CellTemplateContext<Row>` argument rather than positional arguments, so a later addition to it is not a breaking change to every template already written.

| Field          | Type      | Description                                                                                                                                               |
| -------------- | --------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `value`        | `unknown` | This cell's value, read off the column's `field` path. Untyped since a dotted path's type can't be recovered here — a template narrows it.                |
| `row`          | `Row`     | The whole row, for a template that needs a sibling field.                                                                                                 |
| `rowIndex`     | `number`  | Position among the rows as rendered — filtered, sorted, and paginated once `paginated` is on. Not the raw `dataSource` index.                             |
| `datasetIndex` | `number`  | This row's absolute position in the whole filtered/sorted/grouped dataset, unaffected by which page is showing. See [pagination](/docs/react/pagination). |
| `rowId`        | `string`  | This row's id, as `getRowId` (or the position fallback) resolved it.                                                                                      |
| `selected`     | `boolean` | Whether this row is selected, so a template can style itself to match.                                                                                    |

## Styling a template

A class in a template is your own code, so it reaches the page the way any class in your app does. Tailwind utilities work as long as your app's Tailwind build scans the file the template is in. Tailwind v4 scans your project's source files automatically; a template in a file it skips, such as one in `node_modules` or a gitignored folder, needs an `@source` line for that path. The theme itself ships no utilities for your templates.

The theme does register its palette as colours, so `text-accent`, `text-fg-muted`, `bg-selected` and the rest follow the grid's light and dark mode with no colours of your own:

```tsx title="App.tsx"
import { DataGrid, type ColumnDefinition } from "@gridkitjs/react";

interface Deployment {
  id: number;
  service: string;
  status: "healthy" | "degraded";
  cost: number;
}

const deployments: Deployment[] = [
  { id: 1, service: "checkout-api", status: "healthy", cost: 1240.5 },
  { id: 2, service: "billing-worker", status: "degraded", cost: 860.2 },
  { id: 3, service: "search-index", status: "healthy", cost: 92.75 },
];

const currency = new Intl.NumberFormat("en-US", {
  style: "currency",
  currency: "USD",
});

const columns: readonly ColumnDefinition<Deployment>[] = [
  { field: "service", header: "Service" },
  {
    field: "status",
    header: "Status",
    cellTemplate: ({ row }) => (
      <span
        className={
          row.status === "healthy"
            ? "text-fg-muted"
            : "font-semibold text-accent"
        }
      >
        {row.status}
      </span>
    ),
  },
  {
    field: "cost",
    header: "Cost",
    type: "currency",
    cellTemplate: ({ row }) => currency.format(row.cost),
  },
];

export default function App() {
  return (
    <DataGrid
      columns={columns}
      dataSource={deployments}
      getRowId={(row) => String(row.id)}
      label="Deployments"
    />
  );
}
```

Without Tailwind, give the element a class of your own and style it in your stylesheet, reading the theme's tokens such as `var(--gridkit-accent)`. For a class on every cell of a column, rather than on markup inside it, use the column's `cellClassName` or `headerClassName`; see [column definitions](/docs/react/column-definition).

## See also

- [Column definitions](/docs/react/column-definition) for `format`, `valueFormatter`, `cellClassName` and every other field.
- [Theming](/docs/theme-tailwind#theme-tokens) for the palette tokens a template can use.
- [Pagination](/docs/react/pagination) for `rowIndex` and `datasetIndex` on a paged grid.

The grid exports what the user sees as a file: CSV or JSON out of the box, and any other format — Excel, or one of your own — through an export writer. The toolbar's Export dropdown, an export item in the context menu and `ref.current.runCommand("export.csv")` all run the same built-in commands and download the same file; `ref.current.exportData("csv")` hands you the file as a `Blob` instead, to upload or keep.

An export is built from the grid's state, never from its page, so rows on another page, inside a collapsed group or scrolled out of a virtualized body still export. By default it holds every row the filter, search and sort leave, every group expanded, and the shown columns in their current order, each cell's text as the grid formats it. See @gridkitjs/core's [export model](/docs/core/export) for exactly what goes in and how each format writes it.

## From the toolbar

<ExportExample />

`"export"` in the toolbar's items is a dropdown, "Export", of the formats the grid can export now:

```ts
<DataGrid
  columns={columns}
  dataSource={orders}
  selectable={{ rows: "multiple" }}
  toolbar={{ items: ["search", "spacer", "export"] }}
/>
```

It lists "Export to CSV" and "Export to JSON", "Export to Excel" and "Export to PDF" once a writer for those formats is [registered](#formats-of-your-own), then — while rows are selected — the same exports of the selected rows alone. Choosing one downloads `export.csv`, `export.json`, and so on. The dropdown is not among the default toolbar's items, so `toolbar` alone does not show it; list it where you want it. For a different list — a single CSV button, or the exports in a dropdown of your own — list the command ids yourself: `"export.csv"` alone, or a [submenu](/docs/react/toolbar#items-of-your-own) of the ones you want.

## From the context menu

No default menu exports, so list the commands in the areas you want them — `cell` for a row's menu, `body` for the grid's empty space:

```ts
import { defaultContextMenu } from "@gridkitjs/core";

<DataGrid
  columns={columns}
  dataSource={orders}
  contextMenu={{
    cell: [
      ...defaultContextMenu.cell,
      "separator",
      "export.csv",
      "exportSelection.csv",
    ],
  }}
/>;
```

An export from a row's menu still exports the whole grid, not the row; `exportSelection.csv` exports the selected rows.

## The commands

| Command                | Item                      | Shown when                                     |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| `export.csv`           | Export to CSV             | Always.                                        |
| `export.json`          | Export to JSON            | Always.                                        |
| `export.xlsx`          | Export to Excel           | An `xlsx` writer is registered.                |
| `export.pdf`           | Export to PDF             | A `pdf` writer is registered.                  |
| `exportSelection.csv`  | Export selection to CSV   | Rows are selected.                             |
| `exportSelection.json` | Export selection to JSON  | Rows are selected.                             |
| `exportSelection.xlsx` | Export selection to Excel | Rows are selected and an `xlsx` writer is too. |

Each shows in the toolbar, a row's menu and the body's menu, never a header's or a group's, and is disabled while there is nothing to export. They report to `onCommand` like any command. See [built-in commands](/docs/core/commands#built-in-commands).

One export downloads at a time: an export chosen while a slow writer is still writing the last one is dropped, and logged with a `console.error`. A writer that fails is logged, and a screen reader hears "Export failed" — `messages.exportFailed`.

## From the ref

`runCommand("export.csv")` downloads, as the toolbar does. `exportData(format, options?)` exports without downloading, and resolves to a `Blob` of the file:

```ts
const gridRef = useRef<DataGridApi<Order>>(null);

async function upload() {
  const blob = await gridRef.current?.exportData("csv", { scope: "selection" });
  if (blob) await fetch("/api/orders/export", { method: "POST", body: blob });
}
```

`format` is `"csv"`, `"json"`, a format registered through `exportWriters`, or a writer passed as it is, for one export only. `options` go over the grid's `export` option field by field, so a call need only say what differs. It rejects for a format with no writer — register one through `exportWriters` — and when the writer fails.

| Method                         | Returns         | Description                                                                       |
| ------------------------------ | --------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `exportData(format, options?)` | `Promise<Blob>` | The grid's view as a file of `format`, under `export` with `options` over it.     |
| `runCommand("export.csv")`     | `boolean`       | Downloads the export, as the toolbar does. Says whether it ran: not with no rows. |

## Options

`export` sets how the grid exports unless a call says otherwise — what goes in, what the file is called, and how CSV and JSON are written:

```ts
<DataGrid
  columns={columns}
  dataSource={orders}
  toolbar={{ items: ["export"] }}
  export={{
    fileName: "orders",
    columns: "all",
    csv: { delimiter: "auto" },
  }}
/>
```

| Option              | Type                                      | Default     | Description                                                                                                       |
| ------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | ----------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `fileName`          | `string \| (format: string) => string`    | `"export"`  | The file's name; the format's extension is added unless it is already there.                                      |
| `scope`             | `"all" \| "page" \| "selection"`          | `"all"`     | Every row the filter leaves, the current page's, or the selected rows.                                            |
| `columns`           | `"visible" \| "all" \| readonly string[]` | `"visible"` | The shown columns, every column, or these ids in this order. A column with `exportable: false` never exports.     |
| `includeGroups`     | `boolean`                                 | `true`      | Group header rows, when the grid is grouped.                                                                      |
| `includeAggregates` | `boolean`                                 | `true`      | Aggregate values: group summaries and the grand total.                                                            |
| `includeHeader`     | `boolean`                                 | `true`      | The column names as the first row.                                                                                |
| `csv`               | `CsvOptions`                              |             | How CSV is written — `delimiter`, `bom`, `eol`, `neutralizeFormulas`, `values`. See [CSV](/docs/core/export#csv). |
| `json`              | `JsonOptions`                             |             | How JSON is written — `keys`, `space`. See [JSON](/docs/core/export#json).                                        |

A column decides its own part with `exportable` and `exportValue` — see [column definitions](/docs/react/column-definition).

## Excel

Excel export ships as a package of its own, [@gridkitjs/export-xlsx](/docs/export-xlsx), so that a grid which never exports to Excel carries none of it. Register its writer and "Export to Excel" appears in the dropdown, with "Export selection to Excel" while rows are selected:

```tsx
import { xlsxWriter } from "@gridkitjs/export-xlsx";

const writers = [xlsxWriter({ sheetName: "Orders" })];

<DataGrid
  columns={columns}
  dataSource={orders}
  toolbar={{ items: ["export"] }}
  exportWriters={writers}
/>;
```

The workbook holds what a CSV would, with numbers, dates and booleans stored as Excel's own in a format read from each column's `type` and `format`, a bold header row frozen at the top, the columns' widths, and group headers above rows Excel's outline collapses. See its [overview](/docs/export-xlsx) for the options and what maps to what.

## PDF

GridKit ships no PDF writer. To save a PDF, the user prints the grid and chooses "Save as PDF" in the print dialog — see [Save as PDF](/docs/react/printing#save-as-pdf). The browser draws the text itself, so every script reads correctly with no font to supply.

A programmatic PDF is the app's own: register a writer with `format: "pdf"` and "Export to PDF" appears in the dropdown, with `export.pdf` running it. This sketch builds one on jsPDF and its autoTable plugin, named only as an example — any PDF library does. jsPDF's built-in fonts cover Latin text only, so for Cyrillic or other scripts the app must embed a font file of its own (`doc.addFileToVFS` and `doc.addFont`) and carry it in its bundle.

```ts
import { jsPDF } from "jspdf";
import autoTable from "jspdf-autotable";
import type { ExportWriter } from "@gridkitjs/react";

const pdfWriter: ExportWriter = {
  format: "pdf",
  fileExtension: "pdf",
  mimeType: "application/pdf",
  write: (table) => {
    const doc = new jsPDF({ orientation: "landscape" });
    autoTable(doc, {
      head: [table.columns.map((column) => column.header)],
      body: table.rows.map((row) => row.cells.map((cell) => cell.text)),
    });
    return new Uint8Array(doc.output("arraybuffer"));
  },
};

<DataGrid columns={columns} dataSource={orders} exportWriters={[pdfWriter]} />;
```

## Formats of your own

`exportWriters` registers further formats. A writer, an `ExportWriter`, turns the table the grid builds into the file's contents — text, bytes, or a promise of either — and says what the file is; the grid makes the `Blob` and downloads it. Registering one for `xlsx` shows "Export to Excel" in the dropdown, and one for a built-in's format replaces it.

```ts
import { toHtmlTable } from "@gridkitjs/core";
import type { ExportWriter } from "@gridkitjs/react";

const htmlWriter: ExportWriter = {
  format: "html",
  fileExtension: "html",
  mimeType: "text/html;charset=utf-8",
  write: (table) =>
    `<!doctype html><meta charset="utf-8">${toHtmlTable(table)}`,
};

<DataGrid columns={columns} dataSource={orders} exportWriters={[htmlWriter]} />;

await gridRef.current?.exportData("html"); // a Blob of the HTML page
```

A format with no built-in command of its own, like `html` here, is reached through `exportData`, or through a [command of your own](/docs/react/context-menu#commands-of-your-own) that runs it. See [writers](/docs/core/export#writers) for the contract.

## Props

| Prop            | Type                      | Default | Description                                                                  |
| --------------- | ------------------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `export`        | `GridExportOptions`       |         | How the grid exports unless a call says otherwise — see [options](#options). |
| `exportWriters` | `readonly ExportWriter[]` |         | Formats beyond CSV and JSON, by `format`; one replaces a built-in's.         |

The dropdown's name, every export item's label and the "Export failed" announcement are messages — `exportMenu`, `exportCsv`, `exportJson`, `exportXlsx`, `exportPdf`, `exportSelectionCsv`, `exportSelectionJson`, `exportSelectionXlsx` and `exportFailed` — see [localization](/docs/react/localization).

## See also

- [core: Export](/docs/core/export) for the writers and options.
- [@gridkitjs/export-xlsx](/docs/export-xlsx) for the Excel writer's options.
- [Printing](/docs/react/printing) and [clipboard](/docs/react/clipboard) for the other ways out.

`DataGrid` has no live mode and needs none: give `dataSource` a new array and the grid filters, sorts, groups and pages the new rows, keeping its selection and other state, which follow each row by its `getRowId`. This page shows a grid fed by a stream of updates, and what to expect while the rows move.

## When to use it

Use it for rows that change while they are on screen: prices, metrics, job queues, order status. For rows that change only when the user asks, fetch them as on [server-side data](/docs/react/server-side-data).

## A ticking grid

A timer stands in for the feed here, moving a few prices each second. Each tick passes a new array to `dataSource`, keeping the same object for every quote that did not move, and the pause button stops the updates while the user reads.

```tsx title="App.tsx"
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";
import { DataGrid, type ColumnDefinition } from "@gridkitjs/react";

interface Quote {
  symbol: string;
  name: string;
  price: number;
  change: number;
}

const initialQuotes: Quote[] = [
  { symbol: "ALDR", name: "Alder Holdings", price: 42.1, change: 0 },
  { symbol: "BRCH", name: "Birchwood Group", price: 118.75, change: 0 },
  { symbol: "CEDR", name: "Cedar Energy", price: 9.6, change: 0 },
  { symbol: "DUNM", name: "Dunmore Trading", price: 63.2, change: 0 },
  { symbol: "ELMF", name: "Elmfield Retail", price: 27.45, change: 0 },
];

/** Stands in for a feed: moves some prices and keeps every other row as it was. */
function nextTick(quotes: readonly Quote[]): Quote[] {
  return quotes.map((quote) => {
    if (Math.random() < 0.6) {
      return quote;
    }
    const change = Math.round((Math.random() - 0.5) * 200) / 100;
    const price = Math.max(1, Math.round((quote.price + change) * 100) / 100);
    return { ...quote, price, change };
  });
}

const columns: readonly ColumnDefinition<Quote>[] = [
  { field: "symbol", header: "Symbol", width: 100 },
  { field: "name", header: "Name" },
  {
    field: "price",
    header: "Price",
    type: "currency",
    format: { currency: "USD" },
  },
  {
    field: "change",
    header: "Change",
    type: "number",
    cellTemplate: ({ row }) => (
      <span className={row.change < 0 ? "text-red-600" : "text-emerald-600"}>
        {row.change > 0 ? "+" : ""}
        {row.change.toFixed(2)}
      </span>
    ),
  },
];

const getQuoteId = (quote: Quote) => quote.symbol;

export default function App() {
  const [quotes, setQuotes] = useState<readonly Quote[]>(initialQuotes);
  const [paused, setPaused] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (paused) {
      return;
    }
    const timer = setInterval(() => {
      setQuotes((current) => nextTick(current));
    }, 1000);
    return () => {
      clearInterval(timer);
    };
  }, [paused]);

  return (
    <>
      <button
        type="button"
        onClick={() => {
          setPaused((value) => !value);
        }}
      >
        {paused ? "Resume updates" : "Pause updates"}
      </button>
      <DataGrid
        columns={columns}
        dataSource={quotes}
        getRowId={getQuoteId}
        label="Live quotes"
        selectable={{ rows: "multiple" }}
        sortableColumns
      />
    </>
  );
}
```

The `text-red-600` and `text-emerald-600` classes are Tailwind utilities in your own code, so your Tailwind build must scan this file; see [styling a template](/docs/react/column-templates#styling-a-template).

## From a WebSocket

A feed can send many updates a second, and each `setState` from a socket message is a render of its own. Collect the updates as they arrive and apply them together on an interval, a few times a second, so the grid renders once per batch however fast the feed is:

```tsx title="App.tsx"
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";
import { DataGrid, type ColumnDefinition } from "@gridkitjs/react";

interface Quote {
  symbol: string;
  name: string;
  price: number;
  change: number;
}

type QuoteUpdate = Pick<Quote, "symbol" | "price" | "change">;

const initialQuotes: Quote[] = [
  { symbol: "ALDR", name: "Alder Holdings", price: 42.1, change: 0 },
  { symbol: "BRCH", name: "Birchwood Group", price: 118.75, change: 0 },
  { symbol: "CEDR", name: "Cedar Energy", price: 9.6, change: 0 },
];

const columns: readonly ColumnDefinition<Quote>[] = [
  { field: "symbol", header: "Symbol", width: 100 },
  { field: "name", header: "Name" },
  { field: "price", header: "Price", type: "decimal" },
  { field: "change", header: "Change", type: "decimal" },
];

const getQuoteId = (quote: Quote) => quote.symbol;

/** Applies a batch of updates, keeping every row no update names as it was. */
function applyUpdates(
  quotes: readonly Quote[],
  updates: ReadonlyMap<string, QuoteUpdate>,
): readonly Quote[] {
  return quotes.map((quote) => {
    const update = updates.get(quote.symbol);
    return update === undefined ? quote : { ...quote, ...update };
  });
}

export default function App() {
  const [quotes, setQuotes] = useState<readonly Quote[]>(initialQuotes);

  useEffect(() => {
    // Updates arriving between two flushes; a later one for a symbol wins.
    let pending = new Map<string, QuoteUpdate>();
    const socket = new WebSocket("wss://example.com/quotes");
    socket.addEventListener("message", (event) => {
      const update = JSON.parse(String(event.data)) as QuoteUpdate;
      pending.set(update.symbol, update);
    });
    const flush = setInterval(() => {
      if (pending.size === 0) {
        return;
      }
      const updates = pending;
      pending = new Map();
      setQuotes((current) => applyUpdates(current, updates));
    }, 250);
    return () => {
      clearInterval(flush);
      socket.close();
    };
  }, []);

  return (
    <DataGrid
      columns={columns}
      dataSource={quotes}
      getRowId={getQuoteId}
      label="Live quotes"
      sortableColumns
    />
  );
}
```

A poll fits the same shape: fetch on an interval, and pass the rows that come back to `dataSource`, or patch the ones that changed.

## What happens on each update

- **Selection follows the row.** Selection is held as row ids, so a selected row stays selected wherever the update moves it. A selected row that leaves `dataSource` drops out of `getSelectedRows()`, though its id stays in `getRowSelection()`, so the row is selected again if it comes back. Without `getRowId` a row's id is its position, and a selection stays on the position as different rows move through it.
- **Sort and filter apply to the new values.** A row whose sorted value changed moves to its new place, and a row whose value no longer passes the filter disappears. There is no setting to hold rows in place; pause the updates while the user works, as the sample does, or leave the grid unsorted.
- **The page stays put.** An update does not send the grid back to the first page; only a change of sort, filter or row grouping does. A page past the new end shows the last page.
- **Focus stays at its position.** The focused cell is a row and column position, not a row, so a re-sort moves a different row under it.
- **Only changed rows re-render.** A row re-renders when its object changes, so keeping the objects of unchanged rows, as both samples do, keeps a tick cheap. Keep `columns` and `getRowId` stable too; see [performance](/docs/react/performance).

<Callout type="note" title="Updates are not announced">
  The grid's live region announces what the user does, such as a sort or a
  selection, not changes to the data. A screen reader is not told a value
  changed. If a change matters, such as an alert crossing a threshold, announce
  it from your own `aria-live` region, and only that change rather than every
  tick.
</Callout>

## Highlighting changes

A `cellTemplate` can style a value by its row, as `change` does above. To mark rows rather than cells, `getRowProps` returns a class or `data-*` attributes for each row, which your stylesheet targets; see [row props](/docs/react/data-grid#row-props). To show which way a value moved, carry what you compare against in the row itself, as `change` does here: the template then stays a function of its row, and `columns` can stay at module scope.

## Props

| Prop          | Type                                  | Default        | Description                                                               |
| ------------- | ------------------------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `dataSource`  | `readonly Row[]`                      |                | The current rows. Pass a new array for each update.                       |
| `getRowId`    | `(row: Row, index: number) => string` | `row position` | A row's stable id, which selection follows across updates.                |
| `getRowProps` | `(row: ResolvedRow<Row>) => RowProps` |                | A class and `data-*` attributes for each data row, to mark rows by state. |

