- [Row selection](/docs/react/row-selection) for `getSelectedRows` and the selection events.
- [Column templates](/docs/react/column-templates) for `cellTemplate` and styling it.
- [Accessibility](/docs/react/accessibility) for what the grid announces.

`DataGrid` works out what to draw in a memoised [view pipeline](/docs/react#terms), re-renders only the rows that changed, and with `virtualized` mounts only the rows near the scroll position. Keeping it fast is mostly a matter of not defeating those: pass stable `columns`, `getRowId` and `aggregates`, give rows an id, and bound how many rows are mounted at once.

## When to use it

Read this when a grid holds thousands of rows, updates often, or sits in a component that re-renders for reasons of its own. A grid of a few hundred rows in a quiet component needs none of it.

## Keep props stable

The view pipeline runs in stages (filter, sort, group, aggregate, paginate, then the column layout), and each stage remembers its last inputs and result. A stage runs again only when one of its inputs is a different value, compared with `Object.is`. `dataSource`, `getRowId`, `columns` and `aggregates` are inputs, so a new array or function in any of them runs the stages that read it again, over every row, on that render. A new `columns` array also gives every mounted row new resolved columns, so every row re-renders, and a new `format` object in a column builds a new `Intl` formatter, since formatters are cached by that object.

This costs something only when the component holding the grid re-renders. The grid's own changes, such as a sort click or a selection, create no new props.

| Prop         | Keep it stable by                                                                                        |
| ------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `columns`    | Declaring it at module scope, or with `useMemo` over the values it depends on.                           |
| `getRowId`   | Declaring it at module scope, or with `useCallback`.                                                     |
| `aggregates` | Declaring it at module scope, or with `useMemo`.                                                         |
| `dataSource` | Passing a new array only when the rows change, and then keeping the objects of rows that did not change. |

Event callbacks such as `onRowClick` and `onColumnSortChange`, and `getRowProps`, can stay inline arrows: none is an input to the pipeline, and the rows never receive them. A row whose `getRowProps` result reads the same as last time is not redrawn for it.

```tsx title="App.tsx"
import { useMemo, useState } from "react";
import { DataGrid, type ColumnDefinition } from "@gridkitjs/react";

interface Order {
  id: number;
  customer: string;
  total: number;
}

const orders: Order[] = [
  { id: 1, customer: "Alder & Co", total: 1250.5 },
  { id: 2, customer: "Birchwood Ltd", total: 87.25 },
  { id: 3, customer: "Cedar Supplies", total: 420 },
];

// Module scope: the same function on every render.
const getOrderId = (order: Order) => String(order.id);

export default function App() {
  const [currency, setCurrency] = useState("USD");

  // Rebuilt only when the currency changes, not on every render of App.
  const columns = useMemo<readonly ColumnDefinition<Order>[]>(
    () => [
      { field: "id", header: "Order", type: "number", width: 90 },
      { field: "customer", header: "Customer" },
      {
        field: "total",
        header: "Total",
        type: "currency",
        format: { currency },
      },
    ],
    [currency],
  );

  return (
    <>
      <select
        value={currency}
        onChange={(event) => {
          setCurrency(event.target.value);
        }}
      >
        <option>USD</option>
        <option>EUR</option>
      </select>
      <DataGrid
        columns={columns}
        dataSource={orders}
        getRowId={getOrderId}
        label="Orders"
        sortableColumns
      />
    </>
  );
}
```

## Give rows an id

Pass `getRowId` for any grid whose rows sort, filter, page or change. Rows are keyed by their id, so a row that moves (re-sorted, or with a row inserted above it) keeps its element and, if its object is unchanged, skips re-rendering. Without `getRowId` a row's id is its position: insert a row at the top and every row below it re-renders with a different row, and a selection stays on the positions rather than the rows. The grid calls `getRowId` for every row on every change to `dataSource`, so keep it a cheap field read.

## Bound the rows on screen

Without `virtualized`, every row the grid shows is a mounted `<tr>` with a cell per column, so the cost of a render grows with rows times columns. Three props bound it:

- `virtualized`, with `height`, mounts only the rows near the scroll position, plus `overscan` rows on each side. See [row virtualization](/docs/react/row-virtualization).
- `paginated` mounts one page of rows. See [pagination](/docs/react/pagination).
- `infiniteScroll` grows the rows as the user scrolls; pair it with `virtualized` so the mounted count stays flat. See [infinite scroll](/docs/react/infinite-scroll).

There is no fixed threshold for turning on virtualization. A reasonable starting point is a grid that would mount more than a few hundred rows at once; measure on the slowest device you support. `virtualized` measures each row's real height, so wrapped cells and group rows need nothing special; an `estimatedRowHeight` close to your real row height makes the first paint and `scrollToRow`'s first jump land closer.

<Callout type="warning" title="virtualized needs height">
  Without `height` the row area has no bounded viewport, so `virtualized` mounts
  every row anyway and the grid logs a `console.error`.
</Callout>

This grid holds 100,000 rows and mounts only the twenty or so near the scroll position:

```tsx title="App.tsx"
import { DataGrid, type ColumnDefinition } from "@gridkitjs/react";

interface Reading {
  id: number;
  sensor: string;
  celsius: number;
  recorded: Date;
}

// 100,000 rows, built once when the module loads.
const readings: Reading[] = Array.from({ length: 100_000 }, (_, index) => ({
  id: index + 1,
  sensor: `Sensor ${String((index % 40) + 1)}`,
  celsius: 15 + ((index * 7) % 200) / 10,
  recorded: new Date(Date.UTC(2026, 0, 1) + index * 60_000),
}));

const columns: readonly ColumnDefinition<Reading>[] = [
  { field: "id", header: "#", type: "number", width: 100 },
  { field: "sensor", header: "Sensor" },
  { field: "celsius", header: "°C", type: "decimal" },
  { field: "recorded", header: "Recorded", type: "dateTime" },
];

const getReadingId = (reading: Reading) => String(reading.id);

export default function App() {
  return (
    <DataGrid
      columns={columns}
      dataSource={readings}
      getRowId={getReadingId}
      label="Sensor readings"
      height={480}
      virtualized
      sortableColumns
      filterableColumns
    />
  );
}
```

## Large datasets

Filtering, sorting, row grouping and aggregates run in the browser over every row in `dataSource`, and run again only when one of their inputs changes: a sort click sorts once, while scrolling, selecting and resizing a column re-sort nothing. A column resize runs only the column layout stage.

A `cellTemplate` runs for each mounted cell every time its row renders, so its cost multiplies by the rows on screen. Keep templates to reading and formatting; do lookups and calculations once, ahead of the grid, into the row objects.

When the rows are too many to send to the browser, or the server must decide which rows a user may see, sort, filter and page on the server instead. See [server-side data](/docs/react/server-side-data).

## Updating rows

To change rows, pass a new `dataSource` array and keep the same object for every row that did not change, as `rows.map((row) => (row.id === id ? { ...row, ...patch } : row))` does. Each row re-renders only when its object, its selection or focus, the columns, or a grid-wide setting such as `locale` changes, so an update touching three rows re-renders three rows. See [live data](/docs/react/live-data) for a grid fed by a stream of updates.

## Measuring

The React DevTools Profiler, with "Highlight updates when components render" turned on, shows which rows re-render on each change. A change that lights up every row points at a prop recreated on each render, most often `columns`.

## Props

| Prop                 | Type                                  | Default        | Description                                                       |
| -------------------- | ------------------------------------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `getRowId`           | `(row: Row, index: number) => string` | `row position` | A row's stable id: the key its element and its selection follow.  |
| `virtualized`        | `boolean`                             | `false`        | Mounts only the rows near the scroll position. Requires `height`. |
| `height`             | `number \| string`                    |                | Bounds the row area, which then scrolls on its own.               |
| `overscan`           | `number`                              | `4`            | Rows mounted beyond each edge of the visible area.                |
| `estimatedRowHeight` | `number`                              | `40`           | The height assumed for a row not yet measured.                    |
| `paginated`          | `boolean`                             | `false`        | Shows one page of rows at a time.                                 |

## See also

- [Row virtualization](/docs/react/row-virtualization) for `virtualized` in depth.
- [Live data](/docs/react/live-data) for rows that change on their own.
- [Server-side data](/docs/react/server-side-data) for rows too many to hold in the browser.
- [The view pipeline](/docs/core/view) for the stages and the order they run in.
- [TypeScript](/docs/react/typescript) for typing memoised `columns`.

`aggregates` computes a subtotal per group (rendered inline in that group's header) and a grand total over the whole filtered/sorted/grouped dataset (rendered in a footer below the grid) — a built-in reducer per column, or your own function for anything they can't express.

## Compute a subtotal and a grand total

<AggregatesExample />

`aggregates` is a plain controlled prop, unlike `columnSort`/`filter`/`groupBy`/`pagination`: there is no built-in UI for a user to add or remove an aggregate interactively, so there's no `defaultAggregates`/`onAggregatesChange` pair — a consumer declares which aggregates to compute the same way it declares `columns`.

With `groupBy` active, every group header shows its own subtotal next to its leaf-row count — `Region: West (3) amount: 90`. Whether or not grouping is active, a grand-total footer renders below the grid whenever `aggregates` is non-empty.

## Inline vs. a group's own summary row

`groupAggregateDisplay` chooses how a group's own subtotal renders:

```ts
<DataGrid
  columns={columns}
  dataSource={rows}
  groupableColumns
  defaultGroupBy={[{ columnId: "Region" }]}
  aggregates={[{ columnId: "Amount", fn: "sum" }]}
  groupAggregateDisplay="row"
/>;
```

- **`"inline"`** (the default) — text in the group header itself, next to its leaf-row count, as shown above.
- **`"row"`** — a dedicated row immediately after that group's last visible entry (its last nested group or data row, or right after its own header when collapsed), with each aggregate's value in the `<td>` for its own column — the same alignment the grand-total footer's own cells have to their columns. A column with no aggregate spec gets a blank cell, the same way the footer's does.

Every nesting level gets its own summary row under `"row"`, the same way every level already gets its own `aggregates` — a nested `Status` group inside `Region` shows its own row right after its own last entry, and `Region`'s own summary row follows every nested one beneath it.

A group's summary row is presentational: it takes up a real row (`aria-rowindex` counts it, matching a header or data row), but it is never a keyboard tab stop — <Kbd>ArrowUp</Kbd>/<Kbd>ArrowDown</Kbd>/<Kbd>PageUp</Kbd>/<Kbd>PageDown</Kbd> step over it to the nearest row that isn't a summary row. It also never splits from its own group across a page boundary: a page's unit is still the whole group, summary row included.

## The six built-in reducers

Each `AggregateSpec` is `{ columnId, fn, id? }`. `fn` is either one of six built-in names, or a custom function (see below):

- **`sum`** / **`avg`** — numeric total / mean. Coerce every value numerically regardless of the column's own `type`, matching how a `number`/`decimal`/`currency`/`percent` column is expected to be used. Null/undefined entries are skipped, not treated as zero.
- **`min`** / **`max`** — compare via the same type-aware comparator [column sorting](/docs/react/column-sorting) uses, so a `date`/`dateTime` column compares chronologically rather than numerically. Null/undefined entries are ignored entirely, rather than winning by sorting last the way they do in an actual sort.
- **`count`** — every row in scope, regardless of that column's own value — it answers "how many rows", not "how many non-null values".
- **`countDistinct`** — the number of distinct non-empty values at that column.

```ts
const aggregates: AggregateState<Row> = [
  { columnId: "Amount", fn: "sum" },
  { columnId: "Amount", fn: "avg", id: "avgAmount" },
  { columnId: "Status", fn: "countDistinct" },
];
```

`id` disambiguates two specs on the same column — here, `sum` keys its result as `"Amount"` (`id` defaults to `columnId`, mirroring `ColumnDefinition.id` defaulting to `field`) while `avg` needs its own `"avgAmount"` key so the two don't collide.

## Cell alignment

A footer or `"row"`-mode summary cell inherits its column's own `alignment` by default — a `type: "currency"` column's cells align right, and so does its `sum`. `AggregateSpec.alignment` overrides that for one specific aggregate:

```ts
const aggregates: AggregateState<Row> = [
  { columnId: "Amount", fn: "sum" },
  // Renders centered even though Amount's own cells (and its sum, above)
  // align right.
  { columnId: "Amount", fn: "count", id: "Transactions", alignment: "center" },
];
```

Only the footer and `"row"`-mode summary cells read `alignment` — `"inline"` rendering is text inside the header's own cell, not a cell of its own, so alignment doesn't apply there. When two specs share a column and disagree, the **first one** in `aggregates` wins for that shared `<td>`: one cell can only take one `text-align`, and both results still render, comma-separated, regardless of which one's alignment was used.

## Writing a custom aggregate function

`fn` can be your own function in place of a built-in name — called once with every raw row in scope, returning whatever value you want rendered:

```ts
const aggregates: AggregateState<Row> = [
  {
    columnId: "Status",
    id: "openRatio",
    fn: (rows) => {
      const open = rows.filter((row) => row.Status === "Open").length;
      return rows.length === 0 ? 0 : open / rows.length;
    },
  },
];

<DataGrid columns={columns} dataSource={rows} aggregates={aggregates} />;
```

The function always receives the **full set of raw rows in scope** — every leaf row under a group (regardless of nesting depth or that group's own collapse state), or every row in the dataset for the grand total. It is never handed a partial reduction combined from child groups: the built-in reducers are associative, so combining a group's children's pre-computed values would give the same answer as recomputing from scratch, but a non-associative aggregate (a median, the distinct-count example above) would silently produce the wrong number if combined that way. Every aggregate, built-in or custom, is recomputed from its own full leaf-row set at every level — that's also why a collapsed group's header still shows a correct subtotal.

## `footerTemplate`: rendering a column's own result

By default, a computed result renders as plain text — locale-formatted for a number, blank for `null`/`undefined`. `ColumnDefinition.footerTemplate` takes over rendering for a specific column, the same way `cellTemplate` takes over a cell:

```ts
const columns: ColumnDefinition<Row>[] = [
  {
    field: "Amount",
    type: "currency",
    footerTemplate: ({ value }) =>
      typeof value === "number"
        ? value.toLocaleString(undefined, {
            style: "currency",
            currency: "USD",
          })
        : "",
  },
];
```

`FooterTemplateContext` is `{ value, rows }`: `value` is this column's own computed aggregate result, and `rows` is every row it was computed over — the grand-total footer's `rows` is the whole filtered/sorted dataset. A group's own rendering (inline, or its `"row"` summary row) does not thread its leaf rows through — `rows` is always `[]` there — since the raw rows are not part of `ResolvedGroupRow`'s own shape; only `value` is meaningful for a group-scoped `footerTemplate`.

## Subtotals never change with the page

A group's subtotal is always computed over its full leaf-row set, never scoped to whatever page currently shows it. This is deliberate: a subtotal that changed value as a user paged through the same group would read as a bug, not a feature, in any ERP context.

```ts
<DataGrid
  columns={columns}
  dataSource={rows}
  groupableColumns
  defaultGroupBy={[{ columnId: "Region" }]}
  aggregates={[{ columnId: "Amount", fn: "sum" }]}
  paginated
  defaultPagination={{ pageIndex: 0, pageSize: 1 }}
/>;
```

If the "West" region's rows span two pages' worth of siblings, its header shows the same total on both — the aggregate composes ahead of pagination in the render pipeline, so a page is a window onto an already-fully-aggregated result, never an input to the aggregation itself.

## Reading results outside the render tree

```ts
gridRef.current?.getAggregates(); // AggregateResults — the grand total
gridRef.current?.getDisplayRows(); // each group header's own `aggregates` field
```

`getAggregates()` returns the grand-total `AggregateResults` — a `ReadonlyMap<string, unknown>` keyed by each spec's `id` (or `columnId`). A specific group's own results are read off `getDisplayRows()` instead, on that group's `aggregates` field, populated the same way.

### Reactively, outside the grid's own tree

The imperative getters above have no "something changed, re-render" signal of their own. `useAggregateState` subscribes to the grand total through the grid's `ref` instead, for a summary footer or bar living elsewhere on the page:

```ts
import { useRef } from "react";
import {
  DataGrid,
  useAggregateState,
  type DataGridApi,
} from "@gridkitjs/react";

function SummaryBar({ gridRef }: { gridRef: RefObject<DataGridApi<Row> | null> }) {
  const aggregates = useAggregateState(gridRef);
  return <p>Total: {String(aggregates.get("Amount") ?? "")}</p>;
}
```

Read-only, same as `getAggregates()` itself — there is no action to include, since nothing drives an aggregate result directly. It updates whenever a filter, sort, or regroup changes the grand total, even though none of those report a dedicated `on*Change` for aggregates specifically — the same "derived, not directly committed" shape [`usePaginationState`](/docs/react/pagination#building-a-pager-outside-the-grids-own-tree) has for the silent page-0 reset. A specific group's own subtotal is still read off `getDisplayRows()`'s per-header `aggregates` field — see [`useGroupByState`](/docs/react/row-grouping#building-a-group-by-ui-outside-the-grids-own-tree). Before the grid mounts, it reads as an empty map. See [imperative handle](/docs/react/imperative-handle) for `subscribe`, the primitive this hook is built on.

## Props

| Prop                    | Type                  | Default    | Description                                                                            |
| ----------------------- | --------------------- | ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `aggregates`            | `AggregateState<Row>` |            | Aggregates to compute — a subtotal per group and a grand total. Controlled.            |
| `groupAggregateDisplay` | `"inline" \| "row"`   | `"inline"` | Where a group's own subtotal renders. No effect when `aggregates` is empty or omitted. |

`AggregateState<Row>` is `readonly AggregateSpec<Row>[]`. `AggregateSpec<Row>` is `{ columnId: string; fn: BuiltInAggregate | AggregateFn<Row>; id?: string; alignment?: ColumnAlignment }`. `BuiltInAggregate` is `"sum" | "avg" | "min" | "max" | "count" | "countDistinct"`. `AggregateFn<Row>` is `(rows: readonly Row[]) => unknown`. `AggregateResults` is `ReadonlyMap<string, unknown>`. `ColumnAlignment` is `"left" | "center" | "right"`, the same type `ColumnDefinition.alignment` uses.

`ColumnDefinition.footerTemplate` is `(context: FooterTemplateContext<Row>) => Node`, where `FooterTemplateContext<Row>` is `{ value: unknown; rows: readonly Row[] }`.

