`infiniteScroll` loads more rows as the user scrolls near the bottom of the grid's body, in place of `paginated`'s page-by-page navigation. It takes a config object: `hasMore` and `onLoadMore` are required, everything else tunes presentation.

```tsx
<DataGrid
  columns={columns}
  dataSource={rows}
  height={400}
  infiniteScroll={{
    hasMore,
    isLoadingMore,
    onLoadMore: () => {
      // fetch the next page and append it to `rows`
    },
  }}
/>
```

- `hasMore` — whether a further `onLoadMore` could still return more rows. `false` removes the loading [sentinel row](/docs/react#terms) entirely, so no further scrolling can trigger a load.
- `onLoadMore` — called at most once per genuine threshold-crossing: when the sentinel comes within `rootMargin` of the body's visible area, and not again until `isLoadingMore` has gone back to `false`.
- `isLoadingMore` — whether a load triggered by `onLoadMore` is still in flight. Suppresses further calls to it until this goes back to `false`.

<InfiniteScrollExample />

<Callout type="warning" title="Mutually exclusive with paginated">

`paginated` and `infiniteScroll` are two different answers to the same question: how the grid moves through more rows than fit on screen. They do not compose. Setting both logs a dev-time `console.error` and only `paginated` takes effect.

</Callout>

The message reads:

```tsx
// console.error: DataGrid: `paginated` and `infiniteScroll` are
// mutually exclusive — only `paginated` takes effect while both are set.
<DataGrid paginated infiniteScroll={{ hasMore, onLoadMore }} />
```

## `rootMargin` fires the load early

The grid watches its last row against its own scrollable body, not the page, so loading works even when the grid sits in a page that does not scroll. `rootMargin` (default `"200px"`) widens that area, so `onLoadMore` fires while the last row is still `rootMargin` away from view rather than only once the user reaches the bottom:

```tsx
<DataGrid
  columns={columns}
  dataSource={rows}
  height={400}
  infiniteScroll={{ hasMore, onLoadMore, rootMargin: "400px" }}
/>
```

## The loading row

While `isLoadingMore` is `true`, a "Loading more…" row (`messages.loadingMore`) renders above the sentinel. `loadingTemplate` replaces it, as `pager.template` does for the pager:

```tsx
<DataGrid
  columns={columns}
  dataSource={rows}
  height={400}
  infiniteScroll={{
    hasMore,
    isLoadingMore,
    onLoadMore,
    loadingTemplate: () => <Spinner />,
  }}
/>
```

Both the loading row and the sentinel are presentational: neither counts toward `aria-rowcount`, and neither is a stop keyboard navigation's arrow keys can land on .

## Appends only

Unlike `paginated`, which returns to the first page when a filter, sort or group-by change shrinks the result, `infiniteScroll` never resets: it only appends what `onLoadMore` provides. If `dataSource` changes for another reason, the grid checks the new rows on the next render and loads again if the last row is still in range.

## Pair with `virtualized`

`infiniteScroll` and [`virtualized`](/docs/react/row-virtualization) are independent — they work with or without each other. But a grid that mounts every row it has loaded keeps growing its DOM, which is the problem virtualization solves. For a dataset that grows without bound, pair the two (a recommendation, not a requirement):

```tsx
<DataGrid
  columns={columns}
  dataSource={rows}
  height={400}
  virtualized
  infiniteScroll={{ hasMore, isLoadingMore, onLoadMore }}
/>
```

## Props

`infiniteScroll` takes an `InfiniteScrollConfig`:

| Prop              | Type              | Default   | Description                                                         |
| ----------------- | ----------------- | --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `hasMore`         | `boolean`         | —         | Whether a further `onLoadMore` could still return more rows.        |
| `onLoadMore`      | `() => void`      | —         | Called at most once per threshold-crossing.                         |
| `isLoadingMore`   | `boolean`         | `false`   | Suppresses `onLoadMore` until this goes back to `false`.            |
| `rootMargin`      | `string`          | `"200px"` | `IntersectionObserver` `rootMargin` — how early `onLoadMore` fires. |
| `loadingTemplate` | `() => ReactNode` | —         | Replaces the built-in "Loading more…" row.                          |

## See also

- [Scrollable layout](/docs/react/data-grid#scrollable-layout) for `height`, the bounded viewport the sentinel is observed against.
- [Row virtualization](/docs/react/row-virtualization) for `virtualized`, the recommended pairing for a dataset that grows without bound.
- [Pagination](/docs/react/pagination) for `paginated`, the alternative `infiniteScroll` is mutually exclusive with.
- [Server-side data](/docs/react/server-side-data) for driving the sort, filter, pagination and `onLoadMore` from a server.

`paginated` splits the grid's rows into pages of `pagination.pageSize`. It composes after every other row transform — filtering, sorting, and grouping all run first, so a page is a window onto the finished result.

## Enable pagination

<PaginationExample />

Off by default. `defaultPagination` seeds the uncontrolled starting page and page size — omit it and a paginated grid starts at `{ pageIndex: 0, pageSize: 25 }`. `pager.sizeOptions` feeds the built-in pager's page-size `<select>`; omit it (or pass an empty array) and that control doesn't render, leaving the bar of First, Previous, the page display, Next and Last.

## Props

| Prop                 | Type                                     | Default                          | Description                                                                                                                                             |
| -------------------- | ---------------------------------------- | -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `paginated`          | `boolean`                                | `false`                          | Whether the grid's rows are split into pages.                                                                                                           |
| `defaultPagination`  | `PaginationState`                        | `{ pageIndex: 0, pageSize: 25 }` | The page and page size to start on. Uncontrolled.                                                                                                       |
| `pagination`         | `PaginationState`                        |                                  | The page and page size, controlled — changed only through `onPaginationChange`, and never reset by the grid. See [controlled state](/docs/react/state). |
| `pager`              | `PagerConfig`                            |                                  | Presentation options for the built-in pager.                                                                                                            |
| `onPaginationChange` | `(event: PaginationChangeEvent) => void` |                                  | Once when the user changes the page or the page size.                                                                                                   |

`PaginationState` is `{ pageIndex: number; pageSize: number }` (`pageIndex` 0-based). `PaginationChangeEvent` is `{ pagination: PaginationState; pageCount: number }` — the state to persist, plus the page count it produces, so a consumer doesn't have to recompute it. `PagerConfig` is `{ sizeOptions?: readonly number[]; variant?: "compact" | "numbered"; boundaryCount?: number; siblingCount?: number; template?: (context: PagerTemplateContext) => ReactNode }`.

### `pager` fields

| Field           | Type                                           | Default     | Description                                                                                          |
| --------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `sizeOptions`   | `readonly number[]`                            |             | Page sizes offered by the page-size `<select>`. No control renders when omitted or empty.            |
| `variant`       | `"compact" \| "numbered"`                      | `"compact"` | `"compact"` shows a "Page X of Y" status between the arrows; `"numbered"` shows page-number buttons. |
| `boundaryCount` | `number`                                       | `1`         | Numbered variant only. Pages always shown at each end.                                               |
| `siblingCount`  | `number`                                       | `1`         | Numbered variant only. Pages shown on each side of the current page.                                 |
| `template`      | `(context: PagerTemplateContext) => ReactNode` |             | Replaces the built-in pager entirely when given. `variant` is ignored.                               |

## The built-in pager

Turning `paginated` on renders a compact pager below the grid: one connected bar of First, Previous, a "Page X of Y" display, Next and Last, followed (when `pager.sizeOptions` is given) by a page-size `<select>` with its label to its right. The pager is a `role="group"` named by the `pagerLabel` message. The four arrow buttons are icons, named by the `firstPage`, `previousPage`, `nextPage` and `lastPage` messages; the status and the select's label are `pageStatus` and `pageSizeLabel`. All of them can be translated, see [localization](/docs/react/localization).

The bar holds still as the user pages: the status keeps the width of its widest text, "Page 30 of 30", from the first page on, and under the numbered variant every page-number slot is the same width, so the Next and Last buttons never move.

It's semantically classed (`gridkit-grid-pager`, `grid-pager-bar`, `grid-pager-button`, `grid-pager-page`, `grid-pager-status`, `grid-pager-page-size` and a few more, listed in the [theme's pager classes](/docs/theme-tailwind#pager)) rather than carrying its own layout, the same as the rest of `@gridkitjs/react`; style it via `@gridkitjs/theme-tailwind` or your own CSS targeting those classes.

A button that leads nowhere, First and Previous on the first page or Next and Last on the last, is disabled with `aria-disabled="true"` rather than the `disabled` attribute, so it stays focusable. Pressing Next with <Kbd>Enter</Kbd> until the last page leaves focus on Next, announced as unavailable, rather than dropping it to the page, and pressing it again does nothing. First, Previous and Last behave the same way at their own end.

To suppress the built-in pager while keeping row-windowing and the imperative API active, pass `pager={{ template: () => null }}` rather than turning `paginated` off — see [Custom pager template](#custom-pager-template) below.

### Numbered pagination

`pager={{ variant: "numbered" }}` swaps the "Page X of Y" display for page-number buttons, `[«][‹][1][2][3][4][5][…][n][›][»]`, instead of the default `"compact"` display. The example at the top of this page uses it, with `sizeOptions` for the page-size control.

`pager.boundaryCount` (default `1`) sets how many pages are always shown at each end; `pager.siblingCount` (default `1`) sets how many pages are shown on each side of the current page. Once there are more pages than fit, each run of skipped pages collapses into a single `…` (`.grid-pager-ellipsis`), never two adjacent ones, and the window keeps the same number of slots on every page: `2 * boundaryCount + 2 * siblingCount + 3`, 7 with the defaults. Near either end the pages around the current one slide inwards rather than the window shrinking, so page 1 of 30 shows `1 2 3 4 5 … 30` and page 30 shows `1 … 26 27 28 29 30`. [`paginationWindow`](/docs/core/paging) works the window out. Both options are ignored under the default `"compact"` variant.

Each page button is named by the `pageButtonLabel` message — "Page 3" in English — and the active page's button carries `aria-current="page"` rather than a dedicated CSS class — style it with `.grid-pager-button[aria-current="page"]`.

### Custom pager template

`pager.template` replaces the built-in pager's markup entirely with your own. It's a render prop, called on every render where pagination-relevant state changed, so it's always current — unlike building a pager purely against the imperative handle, which has no "something changed, re-render" signal of its own for a silent page reset (filtering, sorting, or regrouping shrinking the page count) or a data-driven `pageCount` change.

Reach for `template` only when `pager.variant: "numbered"` genuinely doesn't fit — it already gives you `[«][‹][1][2][3][4][5][…][n][›][»]` with no markup of your own to write.

```tsx title="App.tsx"
import { DataGrid, type ColumnDefinition } from "@gridkitjs/react";

interface Sale {
  id: number;
  region: string;
  rep: string;
  amount: number;
}

const regions = ["North", "South", "East", "West", "Central"];

const sales: Sale[] = Array.from({ length: 60 }, (_, index) => ({
  id: index + 1,
  region: regions[index % regions.length] ?? "North",
  rep: `Rep ${String((index % 12) + 1)}`,
  amount: 100 + ((index * 37) % 900),
}));

const columns: readonly ColumnDefinition<Sale>[] = [
  { field: "id", header: "#", type: "number", width: 80 },
  { field: "region", header: "Region" },
  { field: "rep", header: "Rep" },
  { field: "amount", header: "Amount", type: "number" },
];

export default function App() {
  return (
    <DataGrid
      columns={columns}
      dataSource={sales}
      getRowId={(row) => String(row.id)}
      label="Sales"
      paginated
      defaultPagination={{ pageIndex: 0, pageSize: 10 }}
      pager={{
        template: ({ currentPage, pageCount, previousPage, nextPage }) => (
          <div className="my-pager">
            <button onClick={previousPage} disabled={currentPage <= 1}>
              Back
            </button>
            <span>
              {currentPage} / {pageCount}
            </span>
            <button onClick={nextPage} disabled={currentPage >= pageCount}>
              Forward
            </button>
          </div>
        ),
      }}
    />
  );
}
```

`PagerTemplateContext` carries everything needed to rebuild the built-in pager yourself:

| Field             | Type                             | Description                                                              |
| ----------------- | -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `pagination`      | `PaginationState`                | Same shape as `DataGridApi.getPagination()` — 0-based `pageIndex`.       |
| `currentPage`     | `number`                         | `pagination.pageIndex + 1`, clamped — the display-ready page number.     |
| `pageCount`       | `number`                         | How many pages the current page size splits the rows into.               |
| `pageSizeOptions` | `readonly number[] \| undefined` | Passthrough of `pager.sizeOptions`.                                      |
| `goToPage`        | `(pageIndex: number) => void`    | 0-based, same as `DataGridApi.goToPage` — not `currentPage`'s numbering. |
| `nextPage`        | `() => void`                     | Moves to the next page. A no-op on the last page.                        |
| `previousPage`    | `() => void`                     | Moves to the previous page. A no-op on the first page.                   |
| `setPageSize`     | `(pageSize: number) => void`     | Changes the page size, resetting to the first page.                      |

Return `null` from `pager.template` to hide the built-in pager while keeping row windowing and the imperative API active. `variant` is ignored when `template` is given.

## A page's unit is a group, not a leaf row

When grouping and pagination are both on, `pageSize` counts top-level groups, not leaf rows — a group is never split across a page boundary. A group header on one page and its remaining rows on the next would read as broken, so a group always stays whole.

```tsx title="App.tsx"
import { DataGrid, type ColumnDefinition } from "@gridkitjs/react";

interface Sale {
  id: number;
  region: string;
  rep: string;
  amount: number;
}

const regions = ["North", "South", "East", "West", "Central"];

const sales: Sale[] = Array.from({ length: 60 }, (_, index) => ({
  id: index + 1,
  region: regions[index % regions.length] ?? "North",
  rep: `Rep ${String((index % 12) + 1)}`,
  amount: 100 + ((index * 37) % 900),
}));

const columns: readonly ColumnDefinition<Sale>[] = [
  { field: "id", header: "#", type: "number", width: 80 },
  { field: "region", header: "Region" },
  { field: "rep", header: "Rep" },
  { field: "amount", header: "Amount", type: "number" },
];

export default function App() {
  return (
    <DataGrid
      columns={columns}
      dataSource={sales}
      getRowId={(row) => String(row.id)}
      label="Sales by region"
      groupableColumns
      defaultGroupBy={[{ columnId: "region" }]}
      paginated
      defaultPagination={{ pageIndex: 0, pageSize: 2 }}
    />
  );
}
```

With `pageSize: 2`, each page holds two whole top-level regions — however many rows each contains — never half of one. Without `groupBy` the units are just rows, so `paginated` alone pages by row count.

Nested group levels don't add extra units of their own — a top-level group's whole subtree (nested headers and their rows alike) counts as the one unit its own top-level header started.

## `rowIndex` vs. the row's dataset position

`CellTemplateContext.rowIndex` (and `ResolvedRow.rowIndex`) is the row's position within whatever is currently rendered — the index within the page, once `paginated` is on. A second field, `datasetIndex`, reports the row's absolute position in the whole filtered/sorted/grouped dataset, unaffected by which page is showing:

```tsx title="App.tsx"
import { DataGrid, type ColumnDefinition } from "@gridkitjs/react";

interface Sale {
  id: number;
  region: string;
  rep: string;
  amount: number;
}

const regions = ["North", "South", "East", "West", "Central"];

const sales: Sale[] = Array.from({ length: 60 }, (_, index) => ({
  id: index + 1,
  region: regions[index % regions.length] ?? "North",
  rep: `Rep ${String((index % 12) + 1)}`,
  amount: 100 + ((index * 37) % 900),
}));

const columns: readonly ColumnDefinition<Sale>[] = [
  {
    field: "id",
    id: "rowNumber",
    header: "No.",
    width: 80,
    // A stable, dataset-wide row number: page 2 starts at 26, not 1.
    cellTemplate: ({ datasetIndex }) => String(datasetIndex + 1),
  },
  { field: "region", header: "Region" },
  { field: "amount", header: "Amount", type: "number" },
];

export default function App() {
  return (
    <DataGrid
      columns={columns}
      dataSource={sales}
      getRowId={(row) => String(row.id)}
      label="Sales"
      paginated
    />
  );
}
```

Use `rowIndex` for anything about the row's position on screen (alternating row styling, "row 3 of this page"); use `datasetIndex` for anything that should stay stable across pages, such as a running row number. It counts rows as shown, so it follows the sort and filter, and in a grouped grid it counts group rows and summary rows too. It is not the row's index in `dataSource`: to find the row there, use its `row` or `rowId`.

This is also why `aria-rowindex` on a rendered row reports the dataset position, not the page-relative one — the WAI-ARIA grid pattern expects a row's index to reflect its true position in the full set even when only one page of it is in the DOM. `aria-rowcount` stays the total row count the same way. Neither of these changes for a grid that never turns `paginated` on: `datasetIndex` equals `rowIndex` whenever there's only one page, so this is additive rather than a behavior change for an existing, unpaginated grid.

## Changing pages resets keyboard navigation and range selection to the page

Arrow-key navigation and <Kbd>Shift</Kbd>+click range selection both operate on what's actually rendered — the current page — not the whole dataset. A <Kbd>Shift</Kbd>+click range drawn on page 1 and then paged away from does not retroactively include rows revealed by a later page, the same way a range spanning a collapsed group excludes the rows hidden beneath it (see [row grouping](/docs/react/row-grouping)).

## Filtering, sorting, or regrouping resets to page 1

A user looking at page 7 of a result that filtering, sorting, or regrouping just shrank to 2 pages is not silently stranded on an empty page — under uncontrolled pagination, any of the three resets `pageIndex` back to `0`. That reset fires no `onPaginationChange` of its own. A controlled `pagination` is yours to reset; until you do, the grid shows the last page that exists. See [controlled state](/docs/react/state#the-page-goes-back-to-the-first-page).

## Keyboard

The pager is native controls: <Kbd>Tab</Kbd> moves through First, Previous, the page buttons (numbered variant only), Next, Last and the page-size `<select>`, and <Kbd>Enter</Kbd> or <Kbd>Space</Kbd> presses a button. The grid's own keys act on the current page only: <Kbd>PageUp</Kbd> and <Kbd>PageDown</Kbd> move by the rows that fit the viewport, not by pages, and <Kbd>Ctrl</Kbd>+<Kbd>Home</Kbd> and <Kbd>Ctrl</Kbd>+<Kbd>End</Kbd> go to the first and last row of the page. See [accessibility](/docs/react/accessibility#keyboard-navigation) for the full keyboard model.

## Building a pager outside the grid's own tree

`pager.template` only reaches the built-in pager's own DOM slot — `DataGrid` still renders it, so it's for replacing that one widget's markup, not for a toolbar or sidebar living elsewhere on the page. `usePaginationState` covers that case: it subscribes to the grid's pagination state through its `ref`, so a component anywhere in the tree stays current with no `on*Change` prop to forward and no polling.

```tsx title="App.tsx"
import { useRef, type RefObject } from "react";
import {
  DataGrid,
  usePaginationState,
  type ColumnDefinition,
  type DataGridApi,
} from "@gridkitjs/react";

interface Sale {
  id: number;
  region: string;
  rep: string;
  amount: number;
}

const regions = ["North", "South", "East", "West", "Central"];

const sales: Sale[] = Array.from({ length: 60 }, (_, index) => ({
  id: index + 1,
  region: regions[index % regions.length] ?? "North",
  rep: `Rep ${String((index % 12) + 1)}`,
  amount: 100 + ((index * 37) % 900),
}));

const columns: readonly ColumnDefinition<Sale>[] = [
  { field: "id", header: "#", type: "number", width: 80 },
  { field: "region", header: "Region" },
  { field: "rep", header: "Rep" },
  { field: "amount", header: "Amount", type: "number" },
];

function PageControls({
  gridRef,
}: {
  gridRef: RefObject<DataGridApi<Sale> | null>;
}) {
  const { pagination, pageCount, nextPage, previousPage } =
    usePaginationState(gridRef);

  return (
    <div className="my-toolbar">
      <button onClick={previousPage}>Back</button>
      <span>
        {pagination.pageIndex + 1} / {pageCount}
      </span>
      <button onClick={nextPage}>Forward</button>
    </div>
  );
}

export default function App() {
  const gridRef = useRef<DataGridApi<Sale>>(null);
  return (
    <>
      <PageControls gridRef={gridRef} />
      <DataGrid
        ref={gridRef}
        columns={columns}
        dataSource={sales}
        getRowId={(row) => String(row.id)}
        label="Sales"
        paginated
        pager={{ template: () => null }}
      />
    </>
  );
}
```

`usePaginationState` returns a `PaginationStateApi`, below. It updates on every pagination change, including the silent page-0 reset a filter/sort/regroup triggers — the case with no `onPaginationChange` of its own, since the change that caused it already reports its own callback. Before the grid mounts, `pagination` reads `{ pageIndex: 0, pageSize: 0 }` and `pageCount` reads `0`.

| Field          | Type                          | Description                                                |
| -------------- | ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
| `pagination`   | `PaginationState`             | Same shape as `DataGridApi.getPagination()`.               |
| `pageCount`    | `number`                      | How many pages the current page size splits the rows into. |
| `goToPage`     | `(pageIndex: number) => void` | Moves to the given page, clamped into range.               |
| `nextPage`     | `() => void`                  | Moves to the next page. A no-op on the last page.          |
| `previousPage` | `() => void`                  | Moves to the previous page. A no-op on the first page.     |
| `setPageSize`  | `(pageSize: number) => void`  | Changes the page size, resetting to the first page.        |

See [imperative handle](/docs/react/imperative-handle) for `subscribe`, the primitive this hook is built on.

## Imperative handle

The pagination methods on `DataGridApi`:

| Method                      | Description                                                                                                      |
| --------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `getPagination()`           | The active page and page size.                                                                                   |
| `getPageCount()`            | How many pages the current page size splits the rows into.                                                       |
| `getPageRows()`             | The current page of `getDisplayRows()` — exactly the rows the body renders. All of them when `paginated` is off. |
| `setPagination(pagination)` | Replaces the page and page size as given. Fires `onPaginationChange`.                                            |
| `goToPage(pageIndex)`       | Moves to the given page, clamped into range.                                                                     |
| `nextPage()`                | Moves to the next page. A no-op on the last page.                                                                |
| `previousPage()`            | Moves to the previous page. A no-op on the first page.                                                           |
| `setPageSize(pageSize)`     | Changes the page size, resetting to the first page.                                                              |

`getRows()`/`getDisplayRows()` are unaffected — they still return every row (filtered, sorted, and optionally grouped), never just the current page's slice; `getPageRows()` is that slice.

