- [Row grouping](/docs/react/row-grouping) for `groupBy` and the group-boundary rule pagination respects.
- [Column sorting](/docs/react/column-sorting) and [column filtering](/docs/react/column-filtering) for the transforms that run ahead of pagination in the pipeline.
- [Accessibility](/docs/react/accessibility) for the WAI-ARIA grid pattern `aria-rowindex`/`aria-rowcount` follow.
- [Imperative handle](/docs/react/imperative-handle) for the full `DataGridApi` surface.
- [core: Pagination](/docs/core/paging) for the framework-free logic.

`groupBy` stacks one or more columns into nested groups, outer to inner: rows sharing the first column's value collect under one header, then split further by the second, and so on. Filtering and sorting run first and are unaffected by grouping — a row filtered out never leaves an empty group behind, and sort decides a row's order _within_ its group.

## Enable grouping

<RowGroupingExample />

`groupableColumns` turns on a groupable header's click/<Kbd>Alt</Kbd>+<Kbd>ArrowDown</Kbd> toggle. `groupByDraggableColumns` turns on dragging a header into the group-by bar — independent of `groupableColumns`: a column can be groupable via its header toggle, via this drag, both, or neither. Neither gates grouping itself: `defaultGroupBy` groups the grid whether or not either is on, the same way `defaultColumnSort` sorts a grid that never turns on `sortableColumns`. Turn both off — the default — for a grid whose grouping is entirely programmatic.

`ColumnDefinition.groupable` and `ColumnDefinition.groupByDraggable` override their grid-level defaults per column, the same way `sortable` overrides `sortableColumns`.

## The group toggle, the group-by bar, and dragging a header in

A groupable header's icon (click it, or focus the header and press <Kbd>Alt</Kbd>+<Kbd>ArrowDown</Kbd>) adds the column to the end of the group-by stack, or removes it if it's already grouped. `ColumnDefinition.groupToggleIcon` (or the grid-level `groupToggleIconColumns`) hides the icon itself without touching the toggle's capability — <Kbd>Alt</Kbd>+<Kbd>ArrowDown</Kbd> keeps working on a groupable header with no visible icon, the same way the resize handle stays hidden from assistive technology while <Kbd>Alt</Kbd>+<Kbd>ArrowLeft</Kbd> / <Kbd>Alt</Kbd>+<Kbd>ArrowRight</Kbd> still resize.

The group-by bar above the grid mirrors the active stack as chips, each with its own remove button. A column whose header sets `groupByDraggable` (or whose grid turns on `groupByDraggableColumns`) can also be dragged straight from its header into the bar — released at a specific point among the existing chips, it's inserted there, not just appended to the end. A column already in the stack rejects its own header dragged back toward the bar — a `not-allowed` cursor and a muted outline in place of the usual one — since repositioning an existing level is the chip's job; see [Reordering the group-by bar](#reordering-the-group-by-bar) below.

```ts
onGroupByChange?: (event: GroupByEvent) => void;
```

| Field      | Type           | Description                                                        |
| ---------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `columnId` | `string`       | The column just added to, removed from, or moved within the stack. |
| `groupBy`  | `GroupByState` | The full stack after the change — the one to persist from.         |

## Reordering the group-by bar

Once a stack has two or more levels, drag a chip to reposition it — the same drag mechanics a header uses to reorder columns, applied to the bar instead. A chip is also its own focusable stop (plain DOM tab order, not the grid's own single roving tab stop, since a flat row of chips has no shared position to keep in sync): focus one and press <Kbd>Ctrl</Kbd>+<Kbd>ArrowLeft</Kbd> / <Kbd>Ctrl</Kbd>+<Kbd>ArrowRight</Kbd> to move it, mirroring a column header's own reorder shortcut. Both fire the same `onGroupByChange` a click or a header drop does.

## Expand and collapse

Click a group header, or focus it (it holds the grid's tab stop as a single unit — see [accessibility](/docs/react/accessibility)) and press <Kbd>Space</Kbd> or <Kbd>Enter</Kbd>, to toggle it. A collapsed group still reports its full leaf-row count in its header; only its descendants — nested group headers and data rows alike — drop out of what's rendered.

```ts
defaultGroupExpansion?: GroupExpansionState; // group ids collapsed to start with
onGroupExpansionChange?: (event: GroupExpansionEvent) => void;
```

`GroupExpansionState` holds only the collapsed exceptions — a group id absent from it is expanded, the same way `ColumnSizingState` holds only resized widths. Build a group id with `groupRowId(path)` (`@gridkitjs/core`) rather than constructing one by hand; each group row already carries its own as `groupId`, in the rows the ref's `getDisplayRows()` returns.

| Field       | Type                  | Description                                                                                                          |
| ----------- | --------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `groupId`   | `string \| null`      | The group toggled, or `null` for `expandAllGroups()` and `collapseAllGroups()`, which touch every group in one call. |
| `expansion` | `GroupExpansionState` | The full collapsed set after the change.                                                                             |

"Expand all groups" and "Collapse all groups" — the `group.expandAll` and `group.collapseAll` commands — are in the default [context menu](/docs/react/context-menu#default-items) of a group row and, while the grid is grouped, of a cell; a [toolbar](/docs/react/toolbar) can list them too. From a button of your own, call `gridRef.current?.expandAllGroups()` or `collapseAllGroups()`. See [imperative handle](/docs/react/imperative-handle) for both, and for `getGroupBy()`, `getGroupExpansion()` and `getDisplayRows()`.

## Building a group-by UI outside the grid's own tree

`useGroupByState` subscribes to the grid's group-by and group-expansion state through its `ref`, for a custom summary sidebar or group-by bar of your own living elsewhere on the page:

```tsx title="App.tsx"
import { useRef, type RefObject } from "react";
import {
  DataGrid,
  useGroupByState,
  type ColumnDefinition,
  type DataGridApi,
} from "@gridkitjs/react";

interface Employee {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  department: string;
  office: string;
}

const employees: Employee[] = [
  { id: 1, name: "Ana", department: "Engineering", office: "Skopje" },
  { id: 2, name: "Marko", department: "Engineering", office: "Belgrade" },
  { id: 3, name: "Ivana", department: "Design", office: "Skopje" },
  { id: 4, name: "Luka", department: "Sales", office: "Zagreb" },
  { id: 5, name: "Elena", department: "Design", office: "Belgrade" },
];

const columns: readonly ColumnDefinition<Employee>[] = [
  { field: "name", header: "Name" },
  { field: "department", header: "Department" },
  { field: "office", header: "Office" },
];

function GroupingSummary({
  gridRef,
}: {
  gridRef: RefObject<DataGridApi<Employee> | null>;
}) {
  const { groupBy, expandAllGroups, collapseAllGroups } =
    useGroupByState(gridRef);

  return (
    <div className="my-sidebar">
      <p>Grouped by: {groupBy.map((level) => level.columnId).join(", ")}</p>
      <button onClick={expandAllGroups}>Expand all</button>
      <button onClick={collapseAllGroups}>Collapse all</button>
    </div>
  );
}

export default function App() {
  const gridRef = useRef<DataGridApi<Employee>>(null);
  return (
    <>
      <GroupingSummary gridRef={gridRef} />
      <DataGrid
        ref={gridRef}
        columns={columns}
        dataSource={employees}
        getRowId={(row) => String(row.id)}
        label="Employees"
        groupableColumns
        defaultGroupBy={[{ columnId: "department" }]}
      />
    </>
  );
}
```

`useGroupByState` returns a `GroupByStateApi`, below. To toggle a single group, give the ref's `setGroupExpansion(expansion)` the collapsed set with that group's id added or removed — it replaces the set and fires `onGroupExpansionChange` with `groupId: null`; `setGroupBy(groupBy)` replaces the stack the same way. A group's own aggregates are on its header row's `aggregates` field, in the rows `getDisplayRows()` returns, rather than a getter here — see [`useAggregateState`](/docs/react/aggregate-functions) for the grand-total equivalent.

| Field               | Type                  | Description                                    |
| ------------------- | --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| `groupBy`           | `GroupByState`        | The active group-by stack, outer to inner.     |
| `groupExpansion`    | `GroupExpansionState` | Group ids currently collapsed.                 |
| `expandAllGroups`   | `() => void`          | Expands every group at once.                   |
| `collapseAllGroups` | `() => void`          | Collapses every group currently shown at once. |

Before the grid mounts, both fields read as empty and the actions are no-ops. See [imperative handle](/docs/react/imperative-handle) for `subscribe`, the primitive this hook is built on.

## Group-by bar visibility

`groupByBarVisibility` decides when the bar itself renders:

- `"auto"` (the default) — once `groupBy` is non-empty, or while a header drag eligible to drop into the bar is in progress, so there's always somewhere to drop the very first column even from a fully empty grouping.
- `"always"` — rendered unconditionally, a fixed drop target and a constant reminder grouping exists.
- `"never"` — never rendered, for a grid driving `groupBy` entirely through header toggles or programmatically.

```tsx title="App.tsx"
import { DataGrid, type ColumnDefinition } from "@gridkitjs/react";

interface Employee {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  department: string;
  office: string;
}

const employees: Employee[] = [
  { id: 1, name: "Ana", department: "Engineering", office: "Skopje" },
  { id: 2, name: "Marko", department: "Engineering", office: "Belgrade" },
  { id: 3, name: "Ivana", department: "Design", office: "Skopje" },
  { id: 4, name: "Luka", department: "Sales", office: "Zagreb" },
  { id: 5, name: "Elena", department: "Design", office: "Belgrade" },
];

const columns: readonly ColumnDefinition<Employee>[] = [
  { field: "name", header: "Name" },
  { field: "department", header: "Department" },
  { field: "office", header: "Office" },
];

export default function App() {
  return (
    <DataGrid
      columns={columns}
      dataSource={employees}
      getRowId={(row) => String(row.id)}
      label="Employees"
      groupableColumns
      groupByDraggableColumns
      groupByBarVisibility="always"
    />
  );
}
```

## Props

| Prop                      | Type                                   | Default  | Description                                                                                                                                  |
| ------------------------- | -------------------------------------- | -------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `groupableColumns`        | `boolean`                              | `false`  | Whether a column's header shows a group toggle, clicked or pressed with <Kbd>Alt</Kbd>+<Kbd>ArrowDown</Kbd>, unless a column says otherwise. |
| `groupToggleIconColumns`  | `boolean`                              | `true`   | Whether a groupable header shows its group-toggle icon, unless a column says otherwise. Purely a rendering choice.                           |
| `groupByDraggableColumns` | `boolean`                              | `false`  | Whether a column's header may be dragged into the group-by bar, unless a column says otherwise.                                              |
| `groupByBarVisibility`    | `"always" \| "auto" \| "never"`        | `"auto"` | How the group-by bar's visibility follows the active grouping.                                                                               |
| `defaultGroupBy`          | `GroupByState`                         |          | The group-by stack to start with, outer to inner. Uncontrolled.                                                                              |
| `groupBy`                 | `GroupByState`                         |          | The group-by stack, controlled — changed only through `onGroupByChange`. See [controlled state](/docs/react/state).                          |
| `onGroupByChange`         | `(event: GroupByEvent) => void`        |          | Once when the user adds, removes, or reorders a level.                                                                                       |
| `defaultGroupExpansion`   | `GroupExpansionState`                  |          | Group ids collapsed to start with — every other group starts expanded. Uncontrolled.                                                         |
| `groupExpansion`          | `GroupExpansionState`                  |          | The collapsed groups, controlled — changed only through `onGroupExpansionChange`. See [controlled state](/docs/react/state).                 |
| `onGroupExpansionChange`  | `(event: GroupExpansionEvent) => void` |          | Once when the user expands or collapses a group, or every group at once.                                                                     |

`GroupByState` is `readonly { columnId: string; direction?: "asc" | "desc" }[]` — each level's own `direction` orders _that level's_ group values (via the same comparator [column sorting](/docs/react/column-sorting) uses), not the row order within a group.

## Scope

Groups sort by their own value, never by an aggregate. Rendering an aggregate in a group's header, though, is not: see [Aggregate functions](/docs/react/aggregate-functions) for the `aggregates` prop, which populates `ResolvedGroupRow.aggregates` (returned by `getDisplayRows()`) additively on top of everything described here.

